WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.280
- So welcome to the Eighth Annual

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.170
Adam and Anne Amory Parry Lecture.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.890
Co-sponsored by the
Departments of Classics

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:16.830
and Comparative Literature.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.730
I'm Ayesha Ramachandran

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.680
and on the comp lit end of things,

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.150
it's my pleasure to welcome you

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.610
and kick things off today
but before I do that,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:28.230
I want to give a big shout
out to Kirk Freudenburg,

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.350
Chair of Classics who is running around

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.540
organizing things here for all
of the wonderful arrangements

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.000
for today's lecture and
reception that will follow.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.430
Before we start, a couple
of practical matters.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.330
Let me invite you to take this moment

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.793
to turn off your cellphones
or at least silence them.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.950
We, this lecture is being
recorded and will be online.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.440
So I just want to give you a heads up

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.610
that we hope you will come use the mics

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:56.020
to ask questions at the end

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.633
but you will be being
recorded if that is a concern.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.350
And finally, there will be a reception

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.150
right after this lecture in Phelps 401

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.890
right across Old Campus.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.520
Anyone with a Yale ID

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.100
should be able to get into the building,

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.470
take the elevator to the fourth floor.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.760
If you aren't sure you
can get into the building,

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.560
just tag on to the big group

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.560
that I hope will be trundling
over after the lecture

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.490
and please stay to celebrate the lecture

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.640
and our speaker today.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.510
Okay, so supported by generous gifts

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.690
from Professor Gilbert
Lawall and James M. Morris,

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.770
the Amory Parry lecture remembers

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.750
the husband and wife pair of scholars,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.380
Anne Amory Parry and Adam Parry.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.330
Both trained in classical
literature and philology,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.340
both interested in wider
issues of literary criticism

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.810
and the history of ideas.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.250
The Parrys were a formidable team

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.970
who ended up teaching at Yale

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:58.600
where he became head of
Classics from 1968 to 1971.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.630
Though they died very young,

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.150
their work had already
established them as leaders

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.760
in Homeric criticism and the
study of other ancient texts.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.660
Particularly Thucydides and Virgil.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.890
It is in the spirit of their wide ranging,

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:13.730
elegantly written and exciting,

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:15.340
ground-breaking scholarship

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:18.560
that the annual Amory Parry
Lecture brings to Yale

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.340
speakers whose work
continues in the traditions

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.913
to which the Parrys belonged.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.470
So it is particularly fitting

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.090
that today's lecture will
be given by David Quint.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.960
Sterling Professor of
Comparative Literature

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.600
and English at Yale.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.040
David tells me that he
remembers the Parrys

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.550
and their tragic death

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.510
and his own astonishingly
wide-ranging scholarship

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:42.170
which ranges from Homer
to Proust via Virgil,

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:46.540
Tasso, Montaigne,
Shakespeare, Cervantes, Milton

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.500
and the Arabian Nights, just
to name a few highlights

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.940
emerges from and embodies
the intellectual traditions

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.620
of the Parrys.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.250
For many of us in this room,

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.580
this is also a moment of
culmination and celebration

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.410
to listen to David give the Parry Lecture.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.720
In both Classics and
Comparative Literature,

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.190
we have learned from
his immense erudition,

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:09.690
as well as his deeply precise marginalia

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.063
on many of our essays.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.360
He has been a sometimes terrifying model

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.200
for the intellectual generosity

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.970
and scholarly rigor of our fields.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.300
To introduce David properly,

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.970
please join me in welcoming
Alessandro Barchiesi,

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.380
Professor of Classics
at New York University.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.633
Welcome.
(audience applauds)

00:03:39.534 --> 00:03:42.484
- Hello there and what a surprise
that I shouldn't be here.

00:03:43.526 --> 00:03:46.200
I don't have a right to speak

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.938
because people know you so well.

00:03:48.938 --> 00:03:51.310
But it's a fine day.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:53.337
So I'm just happy to walk around

00:03:53.337 --> 00:03:57.157
and I noticed the poster of this event.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:01.960
So I thought there's really
something I want to tell you

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:06.550
before you start and
it's actually about me.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:10.150
So also at the end as you might imagine,

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.123
because you all know this kind of event.

00:04:13.123 --> 00:04:17.650
At the end I will say something
like, David Quint rules

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:21.530
Or David Quint rocks
or David Quint is sick

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:25.460
or whatever the language is
in this kind of occasion.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.630
So my final sentence will
be something like that.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.550
David Quint rules.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.630
But you know, human memory is sedimentary.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.760
So let me go back to the very beginning

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.763
of my knowledge of David Quint.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.010
David is now a dear friend of mine

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:44.740
but we don't go very far back.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:47.320
Initially David was a presence in Pisa

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:51.760
where I was a junior professor
of classics and you know,

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.710
we were all free floating
between Pasquale and Armale

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.570
and the only good coffee
shop in Pisa, Cafe Salza.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:04:59.493
You should try it.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.900
Many of us were interested in a project

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:07.340
which was about the
rejuvenation of classics.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.100
So the idea was basically that classics

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.460
was really always behind

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:17.303
the developments of literary
theory and literary criticism

00:05:17.303 --> 00:05:18.773
back then in the 80s.

00:05:20.539 --> 00:05:24.810
I mean the very first important
paper on imagery in Virgil

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.600
by Bernard Knox

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:31.600
came long after similar
studies of Shakespeare.

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:35.970
The very first influential
negative reading of the Aeneid

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:40.940
by Adam Perry was later then
similar experiments on Milton

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.320
and so on.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.480
So there was this idea that classics

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:49.170
should try have more of a
dialogue with literary theory

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.330
and literary criticism

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.810
and when you start your
career it's very important

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:58.690
that you have some kind of
big hope for your field.

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:01.970
What I didn't know is that
one of the interesting answers

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:06.440
was actually a young Professor
of Renaissance Studies

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:11.170
who was walking up and down
with other people like you

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.159
and having coffee.

00:06:13.159 --> 00:06:17.130
The first thing I noticed
about David is that David,

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:20.950
you know how to pick your friends

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:23.090
and the people you talk to.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:27.110
You were hanging out with
people I knew to be brilliant

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:30.240
like Lina Bolzoni, Sergio Zatti

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.030
and you were talking to Francesco Orlando,

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:37.890
the leading light of Pisa
and literary criticism.

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:41.490
You were laughing at the
jokes of John Beatro Conte

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.030
and so on.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.490
But back then, I didn't know that today

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:49.390
we'd have new answers to my quest

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:51.290
and the big shock after that

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:53.843
was the publication of Epic and Empire.

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:57.980
A book that marks a new
step in the tradition

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:02.330
of comparative criticism
because every single chapter

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:07.330
is a protagonist in
discussions of a single author.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:09.910
Meaning that the chapter on Virgil,

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:12.310
the chapter on Lucan and many others

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:15.860
are now central to critical discussions

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:17.950
of Virgil and Lucan.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.650
It was a bit of a shock for me

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.790
because I was hoping
to rejuvenate classics

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:25.950
from the inside out and now I found out

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.960
that someone under the flag
of comparative criticism

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:33.393
could achieve so much
in just a single book.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:38.450
After that I have had more
attention to your production

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.360
and I came to realize that you
are an authority in Milton,

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:49.660
Cervantes, Montaigne, Tasso, Ariosto.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:51.990
So every time you enter a field,

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:56.925
you change the conversation
in that specialized field.

00:07:56.925 --> 00:08:01.500
We all say negative things
about specialization

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.580
but it's also true that
this approach to scholarship

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:10.580
has created very competitive
arenas for discussion

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:15.453
and regularly David is a
protagonist and a leader.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:18.460
So I obviously look forward

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:21.860
to what you're going to say today.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:26.860
I just want to add that David
is actually a rich person

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:29.640
and also a citizen.

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.080
I was surprised to learn about that

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:37.070
because so many leading
scholars I have met in my career

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:42.070
are not really engaging
reality as we know it.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:46.300
Now David has been active in reforms

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:50.080
and serious changes at Yale.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.560
He knows a lot about politics

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.970
and he has a strong sense of realpolitik.

00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:01.410
He was the only person,
the only friend to tell me

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:06.410
that Donald Trump was going
to win the 2016 elections.

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:09.860
You know blue state, in academia.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:13.170
People were not, even if they knew it,

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.350
they would not say that to me.

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:18.580
I am reminded that David's father

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:23.580
was an important scholar
of American history.

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.890
So there are a number of things
that I know about you now

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.920
after reading Epic and Empire

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:32.913
and after meeting you in Pisa.

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:38.743
But the only important thing
is that David Quint rocks.

00:09:39.588 --> 00:09:42.588
(audience applauds)

00:09:58.140 --> 00:09:59.443
- We can go home now.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.923
Very difficult to follow
that or live up to it.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:08.493
Thank you Sandro.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:14.360
I want to thank Ayesha
Ramachandran and Kirk Freudenburg

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.410
for the invitation to
give this lecture in honor

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:23.160
of Adam and Anne Parry
and I feel very honored

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.603
to speak in their memory.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.880
I also want to thank both
Departments of Classics

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:32.140
and Comparative Literature
for offering me this occasion

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:35.970
but also all of the
collegiality and friendship

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:38.443
they have given me over my years at Yale.

00:10:40.073 --> 00:10:44.650
I was, in recent memory, a
convenor of this lecture series

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:46.190
with Christina Kraus

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:50.970
whom I wish especially to
thank for team teaching with me

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:52.849
on Virgil's Aeneid.

00:10:52.849 --> 00:10:54.270
A really precious experience

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:57.430
which is reflected in today's talk.

00:10:57.430 --> 00:11:00.280
This talk is part of
a larger essay article

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.530
which is simply too
long for a single talk.

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:05.160
I've tried to cut it down

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.060
to a manageable 50 to 55 minutes.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.550
It is like other work I have published,

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:15.987
an attempt at a bird's eye
view of the epic tradition

00:11:15.987 --> 00:11:17.660
but it is more complicated

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:19.503
when it's seen down on the ground.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:24.680
Its polemical cast is gonna be clear

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:26.163
from the very beginning.

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:30.003
Does epic have a future tense?

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:35.580
A now familiar critical idea
advanced by Mikhail Bakhtin

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:39.170
relegates epic to an archaic, closed past

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:42.330
and to a fixed univocal meaning.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:46.000
Against this strawman
Bakhtin defines the novel

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.400
as dialogical, meaning
speaking to the present

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.053
and open to the future.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:53.880
It's fine for the novel.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:56.820
It's the kiss of death for epic

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:59.313
and for the most part it's bullshit.

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:03.723
In the written text it's baloney.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:08.940
Beginning with the Odyssey
and looking back on the Iliad,

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:13.700
epic poems look back from a later moment

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:17.320
on their predecessors and
models and in doing so,

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:19.043
they imagine how a later moment,

00:12:19.043 --> 00:12:22.320
a future will look back on them.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:25.740
This retrospection projection
becomes more intense

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:27.860
as epic moves historically

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:30.280
from the Homeric oral archaic world

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:32.820
to first Hellenistic and Roman epic

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:35.300
and then another millennium later

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:38.320
to the writing of epic
poems along classical models

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.100
in the Renaissance.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:43.430
Beginning with the Odyssey
too and then repeated

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:46.460
as a topost in poem after poem,

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:49.800
epic presents a scene
that creates distance,

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:54.100
temporal and historical
upon its heroic fiction

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:56.360
that breaks out of its past

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:58.440
which was never the absolute,

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:03.280
self-contained past wrongly
described by Hegelian critics.

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:05.510
This particular type scene

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:07.780
is the hospitality and banquet

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:10.210
that the Phaeacian king Alcinous

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:12.720
offers to Odysseus at Scheria

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:17.120
in books eight through 13 of the Odyssey.

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:18.700
And the similar welcome

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:22.210
and banquet modeled on the Odyssey episode

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:25.130
that Dido gives for Aeneas at Carthage

00:13:26.213 --> 00:13:29.960
in books one and three of the Aeneid.

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:31.930
To arrive at a Scheria or a Carthage

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:34.400
is the kind of time travel into the future

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:38.390
through a wormhole that
opens out of the epic world.

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:41.860
These episodes project a
world and audience foreign

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:45.380
to its heroic world that is
also an alternative future

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:46.920
or modernity.

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.670
The world of its later readers.

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:52.280
We readers are to see ourselves invited

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:55.080
to the epic banquet and
as part of the poem's

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:57.580
internal addressees.

00:13:57.580 --> 00:13:59.970
It is assumed that we
readers in the future

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:04.970
may or may not share the
heroic ethos of Martial epic.

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:10.000
Ties as it is to an now-archaic society.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:15.000
We are asked to measure our
own values versus that ethos

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:18.240
for better or for worse.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:19.840
This Scheria, Carthage topic

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:22.420
contains some of the following elements.

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:26.240
His host grants to the
wandering hero a safe harbor,

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:28.830
at least a harbor that
initially seems to be safe.

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:31.400
The hero tells the story of his history

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:35.670
and adventures at a
banquet given in his honor.

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:38.890
The host already appreciates
stories of the hero.

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:41.250
The Phaeacian singer Demodocus recounts

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:42.520
the fighting at Troy.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:46.150
The Carthaginians have
depicted Aeneas in the scenes

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:49.973
of the Trojan war on the temple of Juno.

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:53.940
The two heroes weep at these stories

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:56.130
but the hosts have made them into objects

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.740
of aesthetic entertainment.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:03.610
The hosts, the Phaeacians and
the Phoenicians of Carthage

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:07.840
live in a society that appears
less heroic and warlike

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:10.373
but more materially advanced,

00:15:11.549 --> 00:15:15.030
more scientifically and
technologically developed

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:17.060
than the hero's own.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:20.900
The hero is already part of
a past that is half mythical

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:25.320
and as Odysseus himself
described in his storytelling

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.740
it's consigned partly to an underworld

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:30.053
and to partly a dead past.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:36.240
The hero is in fact invited
to leave his own world.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.970
Perhaps to get with the living present

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:41.040
and the way of the future.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:44.209
Through marriage with the local princess.

00:15:44.209 --> 00:15:46.650
Alcinous offers to wed
his daughter Nausicaa

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:49.230
to Odysseus and Dido goes further.

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:51.170
She starts an affair with Aeneas

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:53.433
and declares herself his wife.

00:15:55.030 --> 00:15:58.476
The seafaring Phaeacians
are wealthy sophisticates,

00:15:58.476 --> 00:16:00.840
unwarlike, vaguely effeminate.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:04.750
Their queen Arete seems
to be their real ruler.

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:09.210
They are better at dancing
with a ball than at athletics.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:10.940
Sir Moses Finley speaks of Scheria

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:15.180
as a kind of utopia and
indeed beneath a thin,

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:19.590
poetic disguise Phaeacian
society appears to contain

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:22.370
what Finley has revered elsewhere

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:24.960
to be conspicuously absent and excluded

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:27.810
from the noble households of the Odyssey.

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:31.223
Trade on the one hand and
the art of metalworking.

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:33.960
The two are connected.

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:36.370
From metals were the most
important commodities

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:38.550
of ancient merchants.

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:41.810
The wealth of Alcinous
and his landed aristocracy

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:46.240
officially depends on the
fabulous fertility of Scheria

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:50.590
and one Phaeacian
nobleman insults Odysseus

00:16:50.590 --> 00:16:53.990
by telling him that he
resembles a merchant seaman,

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:58.060
a man of business grasping for profits.

00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:01.050
But it is the Phaeacians themselves,

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:04.640
Nausicaa has told Odysseus
who have no concern

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:08.320
with the bow and quiver but
is all mass and oars of ships

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:10.600
in the balanced vessels themselves

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:13.170
in which they delight
in crossing the Gray Sea

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:16.280
and that should be on
this very long handout

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:18.040
which gives an idea of
just how much of this

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:19.203
you have to endure.

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:24.020
And indeed the Phaeacians
appear to have magic ships

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:26.200
that not only move swift as thought

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:29.880
or as winged creatures but seem
to understand men's thoughts

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:33.600
and purposes and they know
all the cities of men.

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.680
That is, the Phaeacian
ships and their masters

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:39.370
have navigated the entire Mediterranean

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:42.360
and they bear more than
a passing resemblance.

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:45.440
Both in their wealth and in their name

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:48.810
to the despised Phoenecian traders,

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:53.460
harbors of men's goods
who crop up repeatedly

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:58.010
in Odysseus' lying stories in
the second half of the poem.

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:01.053
And also, in Eumaeus' own life story.

00:18:03.313 --> 00:18:06.130
Homer also transfers to Scheria

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:10.430
the metalworking wonders
of the Iliad's Hephaestus.

00:18:10.430 --> 00:18:12.470
Outside the palace of Alcinous,

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:15.820
itself adorned with bronze
walls, silver columns

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:20.760
and golden doors stand
dogs of silver and gold.

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:22.580
Fashioned by Hephaestus

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:25.410
to stand watch over the palace.

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:27.550
Being themselves immortal,

00:18:27.550 --> 00:18:30.550
in all their days they are ageless.

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:33.820
And it's hard to know whether
these dogs are mere statues

00:18:33.820 --> 00:18:36.290
like the torch-bearing
young men made of gold

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:37.850
inside the palace

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:40.190
or whether they are automata

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:42.240
like those in the shape of young women

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:45.980
who serve in Hephaestus'
Olympian Palace in the Iliad,

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:47.113
in Iliad 18.

00:18:48.625 --> 00:18:51.990
The Phaeacian singer
Demodocus sings two songs

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:53.483
about the Church at war.

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:57.470
The second song culminates
with the fighting around

00:18:57.470 --> 00:19:01.613
the House of Deiphobus, the
second Trojan husband of Helen.

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:04.120
In between,

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:08.590
he tells about Hephaestus'
straying wife Aphrodite

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:11.020
and her lover Ares.

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:13.890
Ares who has won her, both with gifts

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:16.223
and with his splendid person.

00:19:18.385 --> 00:19:20.460
Hephaestus traps the couple

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:23.580
in a net so finely
wrought as to be invisible

00:19:25.283 --> 00:19:28.220
and this song defines, excuse me,

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:31.483
he traps them in a net that
is so fine as to be invisible.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:35.250
This song defines a
Phaeacian subject matter

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:37.253
as opposed to the matter of epic.

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.030
Hephaestus' metallurgical technae

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:43.240
overcoming the god of war himself.

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:45.640
One doesn't need to fight a Trojan War

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:49.763
over an adulterous wife,
nor write an Iliad about it.

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.420
The divine smithy demands repayment

00:19:53.420 --> 00:19:56.680
of his epic courtship gifts sorry

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:58.560
and Poseidon agrees to make sure

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:01.940
that Ares pays the adulter's damages.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:05.370
These are how such matters
are handled on Olympus

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:08.100
and in a civilized, wealthy society

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:10.780
like that of the Phaeacians.

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:14.160
The story anticipates the
entrapment of Penelope's suitors,

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:17.140
minus virtuous Penelope herself

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:18.980
through the craftiness of Odysseus

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:21.000
in the Odyssey's second half.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.110
But in contrast, it makes
the bloody ending of the poem

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:27.601
feel like something like a recrudescence

00:20:27.601 --> 00:20:30.470
of an atavistic epic violence.

00:20:30.470 --> 00:20:33.610
It also expresses an all-too human view

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:35.730
of the Olympian gods.

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:40.070
A measure of the skepticism
that may accompany the,

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:40.903
excuse me.

00:20:40.903 --> 00:20:43.660
The materialist outlook of the Phaeacians.

00:20:44.706 --> 00:20:47.423
The smithy Hephaestus
is their kind of god.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:50.310
In the aftermath of Troy,

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:54.750
the Odyssey depicts a world
that is already post-heroic

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:55.870
measuring the distance

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:59.420
of his own early eighth
century BCE audience

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:01.310
from the epic past.

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:05.140
It dramatizes this historical
distance in the Phaeacians

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:07.670
who lie at an even greater removed,

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:12.270
so post-post-heroic as to be non-heroic.

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:14.460
Like many utopian fictions,

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:17.440
they may lie in an imagined future,

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:20.853
a readership of the poem
that is yet to come.

00:21:23.067 --> 00:21:26.760
Entered Dido's Carthage.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:28.740
If Scheria registers what it means

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:30.880
for the Odyssey to come after the Iliad

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:32.810
and its heroic world,

00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:35.830
the Aeneid measures the
nearly thousand years

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:37.590
of human civilization

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:39.740
and in particular the rise of Rome

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:41.940
and of literature in Latin

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:44.270
that separates it from the archaic world

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:47.260
depicted in both Homeric epics.

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:49.740
It does so by presenting
the versions of Scheria

00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:52.080
and the Carthage that
has just been founded

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:55.450
and which will be no more by Virgil's day.

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:57.980
I'm sorry, it does so by presenting

00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:00.630
its version of Scheria in the Carthage

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:03.320
that has just been founded
and that will be no more

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:05.020
by Virgil's day.

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:07.693
History's defeated rival to Rome.

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:12.200
Carthage is nonetheless Rome's double.

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:14.610
The closest image in the poem

00:22:14.610 --> 00:22:16.960
of the rich and sophisticated city

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:19.650
that the future Rome will be.

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:22.480
Both are non-Greek and a linguistic

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:25.800
as well as a historical
removed from Homer.

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:29.190
Dido and her urbane Carthaginians welcome

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.640
the Trojan remnants who
straggle to her shores,

00:22:31.640 --> 00:22:35.140
the heroic world of Troy
but the Carthaginians

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:37.800
are not part of that world

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:41.050
and belong to a more civilized future.

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:43.330
They provide an enthralled audience

00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:45.790
for Aeneas' narration
of the fall of his city

00:22:45.790 --> 00:22:47.780
and subsequent wanderings

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:51.160
and so prefigure a modern
cultured readership

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:53.653
in Virgil's own Rome and beyond.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:59.680
A Carthage model on Scheria
and the Phaeacians however,

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:03.050
already carried with it
unflattering connotations

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:05.760
from ancient critics of Homer.

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:08.080
From within Athens of
the late 4th century,

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:10.280
the reactionary Plato objected

00:23:10.280 --> 00:23:14.000
to Odysseus' praise of the
pleasure and good fellowship,

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.993
the Euphrosyne of the Phaeacian banquets.

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:21.620
Odysseus says it's the
most beautiful of things.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:25.180
He also objected to the
song of Ares and Aphrodite.

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:26.013
In particular,

00:23:26.013 --> 00:23:28.090
in his attack on the immorality

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:31.760
of the Homeric poems of the
third book of the Republic.

00:23:31.760 --> 00:23:34.340
Philip Hardy notes that defenders of Homer

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:36.650
had to allegorize the body's story

00:23:36.650 --> 00:23:39.140
so that Venus and Mars would embody

00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:43.680
the Empedoclean cosmogonic
principles of love and strife

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:47.820
as they do at the opening of
Lucretius' De Rerum Natura

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:49.850
and Virgil himself apparently refers

00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:51.940
to this allegoresis of Venus and Mars

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:53.900
and the song of the nymph Clymene

00:23:53.900 --> 00:23:55.183
in the fourth georgic.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:24:00.330
Classical writers of Platonic
and Aristocratic leanings,

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:02.480
Pamela Gordon has shown

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:05.130
condemn the Phaeacians by identifying them

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:07.680
with vulgar, Epicureanism.

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:10.383
The pursuit of hedonistic pleasure.

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:13.970
The cryptic Phoenecian attributes

00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:16.550
of the Phaeacians in the Odyssey itself

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:20.380
now belong in the Aeneid
to real Phoenicians.

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:23.790
The colonists who have
followed Dido from Tyre.

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:25.650
So it's with the people identified

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:29.090
with despised trade and double dealing

00:24:29.090 --> 00:24:31.280
that Virgil links the associations

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:34.970
with Epicureanism, vulgar and real

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:36.670
that Gordon and other critics

00:24:36.670 --> 00:24:39.870
have identified in the Carthage episode.

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:42.060
These include the city's opulence,

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:44.510
Dido's skepticism about the gods

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:47.040
who have ordered Aeneas to leave her.

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:50.300
Really the gods care about that she says.

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:53.560
And the sexual indulgence
which tempts the hero

00:24:53.560 --> 00:24:55.763
to withdraw from his heroic mission.

00:24:56.760 --> 00:25:01.130
Here too, a female leader marks
the merchant Carthaginians

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:02.313
with effeminacy.

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:06.650
In place of Demodocus, a song
about Ares and Aphrodite,

00:25:06.650 --> 00:25:11.650
Dido's bard Yopus sings a cosmogonic song

00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:14.270
about the movement of the stars,

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.410
the origins of men and beasts,

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:18.410
rain and lightning.

00:25:18.410 --> 00:25:22.910
Yopus' song is a miniature
piece of natural science

00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:26.840
and it pointedly excludes the gods.

00:25:26.840 --> 00:25:31.840
Wealthy, materialess, godless
Carthage's disparaged twin

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:36.220
but has like Remus,
been obliterated by Rome

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:39.030
but which seems nonetheless to prefigure

00:25:39.030 --> 00:25:42.900
and constitute one side of the metropolis

00:25:42.900 --> 00:25:46.330
that Rome has become in Virgil's own day.

00:25:46.330 --> 00:25:50.163
The city that Augustus was
transforming into marble.

00:25:51.010 --> 00:25:53.600
As Aeneas watches Carthage going up,

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:56.663
he sees the foundations
being laid for a theater.

00:25:58.100 --> 00:26:00.590
We are alerted immediately
to the drama of Dido

00:26:00.590 --> 00:26:02.660
that will soon be played out in the poem

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:04.540
with its frequent imitations

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:07.130
and references to Greek tragedy

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:09.960
but the theater is also
a glaring anachronism

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:12.100
in the heroic world of the poem.

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:14.410
A further sign that Carthage stands

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:17.300
for a later stage of civilization.

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:20.423
Urbane, scientific, disenchanted.

00:26:23.070 --> 00:26:24.650
A world of high culture,

00:26:24.650 --> 00:26:28.030
capable of producing the Aeneid itself.

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:30.500
One would not expect to see a theater

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:33.670
in the backward and poor
Italy of the second half

00:26:33.670 --> 00:26:35.060
of the poem.

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:37.080
There too, as in the conclusion

00:26:37.080 --> 00:26:40.490
in the second part of the
Odyssey, the new Trojan War,

00:26:40.490 --> 00:26:44.490
fought in the Italian
boondocks feels regressive

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:48.130
compared to Scheria-like Carthage.

00:26:48.130 --> 00:26:51.750
This backward feeling is both
reinforced and complicated

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:54.267
by Virgil's separating out from Carthage,

00:26:54.267 --> 00:26:55.890
the Hephaestus material

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:58.040
that Homer had joined to the Phaeacians

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:00.920
in the Odyssey and by his moving it

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:03.970
to the Italy of the poem's second half.

00:27:03.970 --> 00:27:07.300
Gold may be everywhere in Carthage

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:11.059
but the divine goldsmith under his name,

00:27:11.059 --> 00:27:14.400
of Vulcan, his Roman name

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:17.420
has taken up residence
off the coast of Italy

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:19.590
where in book eight he uses his skill,

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:23.020
not to make the luxury
appurtenances of a Phaeacia

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:24.580
or Carthage,

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:28.420
but to produce the weapons of Rome.

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:31.340
The episode of Vulcans
casting the shield of Aeneas

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:34.220
directly follows an Italian version

00:27:34.220 --> 00:27:38.270
of Dido's earlier banquet
and its storytelling.

00:27:38.270 --> 00:27:41.480
Evander has hosted Aeneas
at the religious festival

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:44.220
outside Pallantium and recounted to him

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:48.300
the story of Hercules and
the fire monster Cacus.

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:50.570
Where Dido's Carthaginians are rich

00:27:50.570 --> 00:27:52.220
and listen to the post-epic,

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:57.220
and listen to post-epic natural
philosophy of Yopus' song,

00:27:57.350 --> 00:28:00.120
Evander's Arcadians are poor

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:03.490
and his story of Hercules is pre-epic.

00:28:03.490 --> 00:28:08.040
Part of a deeper mythological
past before the Trojan War.

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:11.943
Hercules fights primal, natural forces.

00:28:13.687 --> 00:28:17.490
The contrast to just the
violent primitiveness of Italy,

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:20.640
Italian Cacus is in fact the half beast,

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:25.600
half god son of Vulcan,
Hephaestus himself.

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:28.450
And he makes a curious
double of the shield,

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:30.430
embodying Roman history

00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:34.433
to which the lovemaking of
Vulcan and Venus now gives birth.

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:38.590
Vulcan not only returns to making armor

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:41.520
as he had done for Achilles in the Iliad,

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:43.310
he does so erotically enough,

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:46.990
at the behest of his wife
Venus, on behalf of Aeneas.

00:28:46.990 --> 00:28:50.520
The offspring of another
of her adulterous affairs.

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:53.780
Venus seduces her husband
with evident allusions

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:58.120
to Demodocus' Phaeacian
song about Venus in Mars.

00:28:58.120 --> 00:29:01.500
In addition, Vulcan
manufactures the thunderbolts

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:04.070
of Jupiter himself.

00:29:04.070 --> 00:29:06.220
These constitute a conservative answer

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:08.807
to the science of a Lucretius or a Yopus

00:29:08.807 --> 00:29:12.060
who sings about the natural
origins of rain and lightning

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:16.790
and with us dispels superstition
about divine thunder.

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:19.810
Placed in a firey cavern
beneath the Aeolian isles

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:21.890
next to Aeolia's prison of the winds,

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:24.531
described at the opening of the poem.

00:29:24.531 --> 00:29:27.830
Vulcan's forge is a kind of underworld.

00:29:27.830 --> 00:29:30.060
But it belongs to and predicts

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:33.520
no less than the britches and
sophistication of Carthage,

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:35.580
Rome's modern present

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:38.860
that Vulcan depicts on the
shield he makes for Aeneas.

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:41.700
The aegis under which the
hero will literally fight

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:44.440
in the second half of the home.

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:47.980
Hephaestian skill produces
the superior weapons,

00:29:47.980 --> 00:29:50.540
themselves technological marvels

00:29:50.540 --> 00:29:53.283
that will lead to Roman conquest.

00:29:56.090 --> 00:29:58.140
By splitting the Phaeacian matter

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:00.910
of the Odyssey into
the respective episodes

00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:04.010
of Carthage and Vulcan's forge,

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:08.240
the Aeneas projects two
versions of the future Rome.

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:11.307
They coexist as parts
of the same modern city,

00:30:11.307 --> 00:30:14.700
out of the audience to
which the poem is addressed.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:17.380
They encode different readerly attitudes

00:30:17.380 --> 00:30:19.420
towards its own epic fiction

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:22.000
and towards the role of the poet himself.

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:23.653
Embodied in Vulcan's artifus.

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:27.170
The Carthaginian Phaeacian attitude,

00:30:27.170 --> 00:30:29.660
materialist, scientifically enlightenment,

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:32.020
skeptical about the gods,

00:30:32.020 --> 00:30:34.640
appears to supersede the heroic ethos

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:37.790
that Dido and her fellow
citizens enjoy hearing about

00:30:37.790 --> 00:30:40.100
from Aeneas the storyteller.

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:44.110
Dido offers to Aeneas a
substitute for his martial labors

00:30:44.110 --> 00:30:46.800
and a peaceable enjoyment of domesticity

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:49.193
and the wealth of a commercial people.

00:30:50.340 --> 00:30:53.570
Through Vulcan, Virgil
may insert Rome's future

00:30:53.570 --> 00:30:55.970
in the Aeneid's epic past

00:30:55.970 --> 00:31:00.970
but he also recruits Aeneas,
an epic's hero of ethos

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:04.770
for the military industrial
complex of the Roman state.

00:31:04.770 --> 00:31:07.160
For the interests of its aristocracy

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:09.620
and its new Augustan monarchy.

00:31:09.620 --> 00:31:12.260
A project that requires the maintenance

00:31:12.260 --> 00:31:17.260
of a religious establishment
and of religious fear itself.

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:19.970
The ambition and
sublimity of Virgil's epic

00:31:19.970 --> 00:31:24.800
may need such a project as
much as the project needs it.

00:31:24.800 --> 00:31:28.400
Otherwise, its Vulcan
might find himself reduced

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:33.400
to making toys for Eros as
Apollonius as Hephaestus does

00:31:33.440 --> 00:31:38.010
in the Argonautica or
he makes a horse trough

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:39.533
for Callimachus.

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:43.030
But it is a Devil's bargain

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:45.720
as is the achievement of Rome itself.

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:47.930
The Aeneid evidently does put epic,

00:31:47.930 --> 00:31:49.820
its archaic warrior heroism

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:51.430
and its old-fashioned religion

00:31:51.430 --> 00:31:54.260
into the service of a modern war machine

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:56.460
and its pious warlords.

00:31:56.460 --> 00:32:00.180
Insisting on their continuing
relevance for the present.

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:04.560
Warfare of today may be
infinitely more complex and global

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:07.653
but still war, it's more of the same.

00:32:08.750 --> 00:32:10.823
It is a reading and an
attitude towards epic

00:32:10.823 --> 00:32:14.170
that Virgil knowingly
transmits to his present

00:32:14.170 --> 00:32:15.567
and beyond it.

00:32:15.567 --> 00:32:17.300
And it may be for that reason

00:32:17.300 --> 00:32:18.933
he wanted to burn the Aeneid.

00:32:19.880 --> 00:32:21.240
His sympathies may lie

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:24.000
with the Carthaginian
Phaeacian sensibility

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:27.840
that the Aeneid censures for
its unbelief and effeminacy

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:30.510
and depicts under historical erasure,

00:32:30.510 --> 00:32:35.330
overcome and obliterated
by Roman military success.

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:38.980
But that sensibility
persists inside the poem

00:32:38.980 --> 00:32:40.892
to be recuperated,

00:32:40.892 --> 00:32:42.792
in part in the poet's own day,

00:32:42.792 --> 00:32:47.563
more fully perhaps by readers
in a future still to come.

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:52.300
Now like Virgil, Renaissance
poets inherited epic

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:55.190
after a long historical lapse.

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:58.740
They took up the classical
genre 1500 years later

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:01.130
in their own vernacular literary languages

00:33:01.130 --> 00:33:03.930
and an age we shaped by
new mercantile capitalism,

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:06.870
new global and astronomical discoveries,

00:33:06.870 --> 00:33:10.440
new technologies of
gunpowder and navigation,

00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:13.940
new science and new secular attitudes

00:33:13.940 --> 00:33:17.120
that accompanied all these developments.

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:19.990
Renaissance epic poets repeatedly stage

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:23.450
their modern distance from
the heroic world and ethos

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:26.100
that they depict by rewriting versions

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:29.440
of the Phaeacian
Carthaginian banquet scene.

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:32.030
As Virgil does in his
depiction of Carthage,

00:33:32.030 --> 00:33:34.680
the Renaissance poet can take a critical,

00:33:34.680 --> 00:33:39.680
even overtly hostile view
towards the modernity

00:33:39.820 --> 00:33:43.983
of his own world,
materialistic and unheroic.

00:33:44.990 --> 00:33:48.130
What may seem to prevent the
possibility of the heroic

00:33:48.130 --> 00:33:50.470
in the poet's own present.

00:33:50.470 --> 00:33:53.440
But the poet also, not so secretly,

00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:57.090
registers an advance over the heroic past

00:33:57.090 --> 00:34:00.620
that allows a hearing for other values.

00:34:00.620 --> 00:34:03.303
Secular, scientific, peaceable.

00:34:04.300 --> 00:34:08.570
Ariosto and Camoes
provide contrasting cases.

00:34:08.570 --> 00:34:12.400
And Ariosto's Orlando Furioso
has the most elaborate

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:15.250
of these reworkings of
the Phaeacian episode

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:17.303
and therefore I can't really include it.

00:34:18.460 --> 00:34:21.593
I'm going to have to just
briefly summarize it.

00:34:22.970 --> 00:34:27.460
The paladin Ronaldo, I've
summarized it on the page

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:29.433
that I just put down there.

00:34:30.642 --> 00:34:35.060
The paladin Ronaldo in the eight century

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:39.607
is hosted in Manchua and
given a banquet dinner

00:34:42.516 --> 00:34:46.880
and then is given a ship
to take him down the Po.

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:48.490
And as he goes down the Po,

00:34:48.490 --> 00:34:53.490
he comes to what is the
site of the future Ferrara

00:34:53.800 --> 00:34:56.200
which where among other things,

00:34:56.200 --> 00:35:01.200
an island in the Po which
had been turned into

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:04.909
a pleasure garden by the Este,

00:35:04.909 --> 00:35:06.990
Ariosto's patrons and which is compared

00:35:06.990 --> 00:35:09.963
and this is in your handout, to, Nausicaa

00:35:11.822 --> 00:35:14.071
and Alcinous would not,

00:35:14.071 --> 00:35:17.610
it's even better than
anything that they had

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:22.563
which is a tip-off to the
origins of the episode.

00:35:23.469 --> 00:35:25.310
At the same time,

00:35:25.310 --> 00:35:28.680
Ronaldo hears two stories of adultery

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:31.200
and about adulterous wives and husbands.

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:32.540
In both cases,

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:37.540
the wives are seduced
by riches into adultery.

00:35:37.797 --> 00:35:41.770
Now the second case is the,
it is the wife of a judge.

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:44.760
So we've come down into the
sphere of the bourgeoisie.

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:49.342
She is seduced by a gold defecating dog

00:35:49.342 --> 00:35:53.253
and he is in turn seduced by,

00:35:54.393 --> 00:35:59.393
and gives up his own body
for a splendid palace.

00:36:04.570 --> 00:36:07.510
The Mantuan setting allows Ariosto also

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:11.538
to praise the Duchess Isabella d'Este,

00:36:11.538 --> 00:36:15.004
the sister of his Ferraraisai patrons

00:36:15.004 --> 00:36:18.060
and other female rulers.

00:36:18.060 --> 00:36:20.090
Versions of Arrate.

00:36:20.090 --> 00:36:23.950
So this modern world contrasts,

00:36:23.950 --> 00:36:27.100
is projected out of the eighth century

00:36:27.100 --> 00:36:30.943
and it looks very much like
the modern Italian city-state.

00:36:31.903 --> 00:36:35.780
It's morally compromised

00:36:35.780 --> 00:36:40.330
as these stories of adultery suggest

00:36:40.330 --> 00:36:42.930
and they're of course modeled on the Ares

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:45.053
and Aphrodite story originally.

00:36:45.958 --> 00:36:48.706
But it has its advantages.

00:36:48.706 --> 00:36:53.210
Splendid palaces filled
with Renaissance artwork

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:55.073
and patrons for the poet.

00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:59.320
So if the Orlando Furioso
finally catches up

00:36:59.320 --> 00:37:02.970
to this modern world at
its end, and this is the,

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:04.420
it is at the very end of the poem

00:37:04.420 --> 00:37:05.963
I should've said as well.

00:37:07.070 --> 00:37:10.020
The Lusiads of Camoes have been there

00:37:10.020 --> 00:37:13.150
almost from the start and never leave it.

00:37:13.150 --> 00:37:16.050
After the initial skirmish
between Vasco da Gama's crew

00:37:16.050 --> 00:37:17.910
and the inhabitants of Mozambique,

00:37:17.910 --> 00:37:21.270
almost all the ensuing
episodes of the poem

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:25.380
are versions of the stay of
Aeneas in Virgil's Carthage.

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:28.110
In Canto 2, Mercury
descends to warn da Gama

00:37:28.110 --> 00:37:31.160
to flee away from Mombasa
as he had warned Aeneas

00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:33.530
to leave Dido in the Aeneid.

00:37:33.530 --> 00:37:35.320
The Portuguese make for Melendi

00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:37.260
where their ambassador first echoes

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:41.330
Virgil's Ilioneus to Dido
and then invokes the example

00:37:41.330 --> 00:37:44.336
of the hospitality of Alcinous.

00:37:44.336 --> 00:37:47.910
The Melendian king is
a Dido Alcinous figure

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:49.550
who welcomes the Portuguese

00:37:49.550 --> 00:37:52.670
and listens to da Gama
for the next three Cantos

00:37:52.670 --> 00:37:55.910
tell narratives about
Portuguese heroism in the past

00:37:55.910 --> 00:38:00.550
and about its national wars
against Islam and Spain

00:38:00.550 --> 00:38:04.560
and then about his own
relatively uneventful voyage.

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:07.400
The storm of book six
drives da Gama to Calicut

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:09.630
much as the storm of
book one of the Aeneid

00:38:09.630 --> 00:38:12.620
drives Aeneas to Carthage and Calicut too,

00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:13.930
turns out to be a Carthage

00:38:13.930 --> 00:38:16.050
from which the hero must escape

00:38:16.050 --> 00:38:19.560
to the warning of the
North African Moor Mansaide

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:22.750
who is privy to the plots
of the Muslim traders

00:38:22.750 --> 00:38:24.760
against the Portuguese.

00:38:24.760 --> 00:38:26.360
Mansaide that is,

00:38:26.360 --> 00:38:29.410
plays the role of Mercury
admonishing Aeneas

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:31.700
to leave Dido's city.

00:38:31.700 --> 00:38:33.890
The island of love of the last two Cantos

00:38:33.890 --> 00:38:36.440
is introduced to us as
still another Carthage.

00:38:36.440 --> 00:38:39.930
Venus had once invoked such
a device, the poet declares

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:42.720
to greet Anchises' son
Aeneas in that meadow

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:47.070
where Dido's subtlety bought
Carthage with a bull's hide.

00:38:47.070 --> 00:38:50.240
The Portuguese enjoy a sexual
interlude there in book nine

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:51.940
where they join all other,

00:38:51.940 --> 00:38:55.980
where they join all other
lovers including adulteresses

00:38:55.980 --> 00:38:58.253
who are ensnared in Vulcan's net.

00:38:59.330 --> 00:39:01.660
In the last, 10th Canto
they are entertained

00:39:01.660 --> 00:39:04.220
by a siren who sings of things not known

00:39:04.220 --> 00:39:05.610
to Yocus of Carthage,

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:08.320
nor to Demodocus among the Phaeacians.

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:11.570
First a prophecy of future Portuguese wars

00:39:11.570 --> 00:39:12.710
and victories in Asia.

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:14.570
This is the Demodocus part.

00:39:14.570 --> 00:39:17.280
Then as her place is taken by Tethus,

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:21.700
a Yocus-like lesson in
cosmography which uses, as a prop,

00:39:21.700 --> 00:39:23.370
a scale model of the universe

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:25.140
enclosed in a crystal sphere

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:28.380
based on a similar
sphere made by Archimedes

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:31.653
and celebrated by Cicero
and the poet Claudian.

00:39:32.860 --> 00:39:37.120
Camoes conveniently labels
all of these episodes

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:38.960
for the reader to make sure that we know

00:39:38.960 --> 00:39:41.080
where we are in the epic tradition

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:43.900
and it seems that we never leave Carthage

00:39:43.900 --> 00:39:48.160
although poem alternates between
bad, inhospitable Carthages

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:52.140
that will be destroyed
and good, hospital ones.

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:56.040
The Lusiadas thus plays almost
all of its narrative present

00:39:56.040 --> 00:40:00.830
in one or other of these
versions of Carthage, Scheria.

00:40:00.830 --> 00:40:04.060
The locus of unheroic modernity.

00:40:04.060 --> 00:40:08.350
And these appear to crowd
conventional martial action

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:12.400
into the past and practically
out of da Gama's mission

00:40:12.400 --> 00:40:14.430
which is at once a failed event

00:40:14.430 --> 00:40:16.900
to set up a trading pact in Calicut

00:40:16.900 --> 00:40:20.230
and a successful voyage of reconnaissance.

00:40:20.230 --> 00:40:22.820
He charts out the sea route to India,

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:26.460
knowing that the Portuguese
will return with their warships

00:40:26.460 --> 00:40:29.750
and establish an Indian Ocean Empire.

00:40:29.750 --> 00:40:31.580
For it is the Portuguese,

00:40:31.580 --> 00:40:34.980
instead of their various
hosts who are the true moderns

00:40:34.980 --> 00:40:37.400
of the poem which recounts events

00:40:37.400 --> 00:40:39.860
that were only two generations removed

00:40:39.860 --> 00:40:42.800
when Camoes began writing about them.

00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:44.710
They have guns and at Melinde

00:40:44.710 --> 00:40:47.900
they give a demonstration
as a supposed celebration

00:40:47.900 --> 00:40:49.870
and salute to the Sultan.

00:40:49.870 --> 00:40:53.030
It was like the Cyclopes at the Smithy

00:40:53.030 --> 00:40:55.860
as the firebombs were exploded.

00:40:55.860 --> 00:40:58.920
These are the new thunderbolts
from Vulcan's forge

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:01.420
that make ancient warfare obsolete

00:41:01.420 --> 00:41:03.960
as the arms made by Vulcan in the Aeneid

00:41:03.960 --> 00:41:06.600
had themselves represented
the modern weaponry

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:09.000
that would build Rome's Imperial might

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:11.310
and would relegate Homeric warfare

00:41:11.310 --> 00:41:14.130
to a distant, heroic past.

00:41:14.130 --> 00:41:18.110
So Portugal, the poet
announces will be a new Rome

00:41:18.110 --> 00:41:21.640
but on a much larger, global scale.

00:41:21.640 --> 00:41:24.360
But Portugal's empire
will be an empire of trade

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:28.690
and this Rome itself made
the war like a Carthage.

00:41:28.690 --> 00:41:31.240
The Portuguese possessed
the maritime technology

00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:35.140
and skills of navigation that
make them the new Phaeacians,

00:41:35.140 --> 00:41:37.550
Phoenicians with magic ships

00:41:37.550 --> 00:41:40.490
capable of reaching all destinations.

00:41:40.490 --> 00:41:41.900
Camoes celebrates a Portugal

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:43.870
that has transformed the globe into

00:41:43.870 --> 00:41:45.830
a large commercial network

00:41:45.830 --> 00:41:48.470
where beneath the superficial differences,

00:41:48.470 --> 00:41:51.023
all ports of call are the same.

00:41:52.086 --> 00:41:54.993
A Carthage that is
everywhere, starting at home.

00:41:57.787 --> 00:42:00.049
The closing stanzas, 99.

00:42:00.049 --> 00:42:04.800
I'm sorry, of book eight of The Lusiads

00:42:04.800 --> 00:42:09.220
draw on Ariosto's language
in Canto 43 of The Furioso.

00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:11.080
To invey in their own turn

00:42:11.080 --> 00:42:13.950
against modern greed and corruption

00:42:13.950 --> 00:42:16.050
but where Ariosto sees
his own moneyed world

00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:18.530
as possibly saner and more peaceable

00:42:18.530 --> 00:42:20.840
than the heroic world of his poem,

00:42:20.840 --> 00:42:24.710
the soldier Camoes wonders
if his vocation is gone.

00:42:24.710 --> 00:42:28.520
Heroism emptied out or
tainted by mercenary aims

00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:31.570
in Portugal's great imperial venture.

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:35.040
Beneath the poem's notorious
double time scheme.

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:38.330
Sorry, not double time scheme.

00:42:38.330 --> 00:42:42.600
Beneath the poem's notorious
double divine machinery

00:42:42.600 --> 00:42:46.290
of a Christian God to whom da Gama prays

00:42:46.290 --> 00:42:49.150
and of classical gods who respond,

00:42:49.150 --> 00:42:53.510
lies a disenchanted world
of all too human motivation.

00:42:53.510 --> 00:42:56.320
Muslim traders bribe The Catual,

00:42:56.320 --> 00:42:59.560
the Minister of Calicut to prevent da Gama

00:42:59.560 --> 00:43:02.750
from leaving Calicut and
to scuttles his mission.

00:43:02.750 --> 00:43:05.930
Da Gama's main Aeneas-like
act in the poem,

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:08.450
Michael Merne and Timothy
Hampton have shown

00:43:08.450 --> 00:43:11.340
is simply to extricate
himself out of this Carthage

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:13.000
in the nick of time.

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:14.720
An Arab fleet from Mecca,

00:43:14.720 --> 00:43:17.381
armed with cannonry of their own,

00:43:17.381 --> 00:43:21.510
Vulcan's thunderbolts
as the poem calls them,

00:43:21.510 --> 00:43:24.370
is due to arrive on the scene.

00:43:24.370 --> 00:43:26.840
But da Gama has really no place to go

00:43:26.840 --> 00:43:29.160
except to another Carthage.

00:43:29.160 --> 00:43:32.673
He and his poet are trapped
in their own modernity.

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:37.150
So Tasso's version of the
Phaeacian Carthaginian banquet

00:43:37.150 --> 00:43:40.250
in the Gerusalemme liberata occurs

00:43:40.250 --> 00:43:43.730
in the episode of the
Magus or Sage of Ascalon

00:43:43.730 --> 00:43:45.730
who alerted by Peter the Hermit,

00:43:45.730 --> 00:43:47.940
invites the Crusaders, Carlo and Ubaldo

00:43:47.940 --> 00:43:51.730
into his underground dwelling in Canto 14.

00:43:51.730 --> 00:43:54.820
The basic shape of the
topos can be discerned.

00:43:54.820 --> 00:43:56.800
The magus offers them a meal

00:43:56.800 --> 00:43:59.300
in his splendid palace which is a product

00:43:59.300 --> 00:44:03.090
of untold wealth and a
locus of natural philosophy.

00:44:03.090 --> 00:44:04.870
And in inset narration,

00:44:04.870 --> 00:44:08.730
he recounts to them the
story of Armida and Rinaldo.

00:44:08.730 --> 00:44:11.050
The next morning he
sends them on their way

00:44:11.050 --> 00:44:14.540
to rescue Rinaldo on the
marvelous ship of Fortune

00:44:14.540 --> 00:44:18.083
which is a version of the
Phaeacians' magical ships.

00:44:18.985 --> 00:44:20.510
But the pattern is complicated

00:44:20.510 --> 00:44:22.960
by Tasso's further modeling of the episode

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:25.000
of the descent beneath the earth

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:27.952
of Vergil's Aristaeus
and the fourth Georgic

00:44:27.952 --> 00:44:31.635
on a more recent invitation
of that Vergillian episode

00:44:31.635 --> 00:44:34.853
and Fracastoro's short
scientific epic, Syphilis.

00:44:37.710 --> 00:44:40.583
The magus lives, sorry.

00:44:42.372 --> 00:44:47.372
The magus lives in a realm
at the source of rivers

00:44:47.430 --> 00:44:50.480
and lower down, of living silver, mercury

00:44:50.480 --> 00:44:53.590
and sparkling gemstones
that fills its cavern

00:44:53.590 --> 00:44:55.350
with artificial light.

00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:57.520
No practitioner of black magic,

00:44:57.520 --> 00:44:59.790
he is a kind of natural scientist

00:44:59.790 --> 00:45:02.867
who splits his time between
the subterranean palace

00:45:02.867 --> 00:45:07.690
and an observatory on Mount
Carmel where he watches

00:45:07.690 --> 00:45:11.550
Venus and Mars without any vale.

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:13.970
As well as the slow or rapid orbits

00:45:13.970 --> 00:45:15.630
of the heavenly bodies.

00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:18.700
He looks down on the
generation of the rain in dew

00:45:18.700 --> 00:45:22.600
and upon how lightning is
kindled and strikes the earth.

00:45:22.600 --> 00:45:24.340
The science of the magus can be linked

00:45:24.340 --> 00:45:27.910
to Epicurean attitudes
and it is he who recounts,

00:45:27.910 --> 00:45:28.750
to be sure,

00:45:28.750 --> 00:45:33.250
with disapproval the song
of Armida's artificial siren

00:45:33.250 --> 00:45:37.410
who advises Rinaldo to enjoy his body.

00:45:37.410 --> 00:45:41.660
Let him care nothing if the
heavens thunder or lightning.

00:45:41.660 --> 00:45:45.820
Let it menace and kindle
bolts as much as it likes.

00:45:45.820 --> 00:45:48.980
Such thunder and lightning,
an Epicurean would explain

00:45:48.980 --> 00:45:51.290
has nothing to do with the gods.

00:45:51.290 --> 00:45:53.600
The meal and the sumptuous
underground palace

00:45:53.600 --> 00:45:57.303
of the magus recalls the
banquets of Carthage and Scheria.

00:45:58.870 --> 00:46:00.890
His establishment in the womb of the earth

00:46:00.890 --> 00:46:05.890
is a scientific version mediated
through Fracastoro's poem

00:46:06.260 --> 00:46:08.160
of the very treasure vaults found

00:46:08.160 --> 00:46:11.340
in previous Italian heroic poems.

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:14.600
Riches, science and
technology come together

00:46:14.600 --> 00:46:16.620
in this projection of modernity,

00:46:16.620 --> 00:46:19.240
apparently far from the 11th century world

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:20.823
of the First Crusade.

00:46:22.010 --> 00:46:24.970
So does the spectra of
a godless materialism

00:46:24.970 --> 00:46:27.590
that Tasso tries to put to rest.

00:46:27.590 --> 00:46:29.670
The magus was born a pagan,

00:46:29.670 --> 00:46:31.410
thought he knew all that there was to know

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:33.140
about the workings of nature,

00:46:33.140 --> 00:46:35.920
but then he met Peter
the Hermit who converted

00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:37.500
and baptized him.

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:41.900
He is another man in parte
than the one he used to be.

00:46:41.900 --> 00:46:42.830
I'd watch out for that

00:46:42.830 --> 00:46:45.010
because that's what
Petrarch says about himself.

00:46:45.010 --> 00:46:46.620
The opening of the Canzoniere.

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:51.003
I'm another man in parte
for what I used to be.

00:46:52.930 --> 00:46:54.550
And it's the magus, suitably enough,

00:46:54.550 --> 00:46:56.030
given his realm of metals,

00:46:56.030 --> 00:46:58.160
who will play Vulcan and provide Rinaldo

00:46:58.160 --> 00:47:00.900
with the epic shield on
which his Este ancestors

00:47:00.900 --> 00:47:02.646
are depicted.

00:47:02.646 --> 00:47:06.146
(speaks foreign language)

00:47:08.441 --> 00:47:10.820
For the blood of the
Este, glorious and aghast,

00:47:10.820 --> 00:47:13.480
the order was seen without interruption.

00:47:13.480 --> 00:47:16.740
By fortunate puns, Actium and Augustus,

00:47:16.740 --> 00:47:20.370
viewed as the climax
of Virgil's description

00:47:20.370 --> 00:47:23.689
of the Shield of Aeneas begin the ecresus

00:47:23.689 --> 00:47:25.763
of Rinaldo's shield.

00:47:25.763 --> 00:47:27.480
If the epics like the
models of Homer's Scheria

00:47:27.480 --> 00:47:29.860
and Virgil's Carthage affords a glimpse

00:47:29.860 --> 00:47:33.270
of a seemingly post-epic
scientific modernity

00:47:33.270 --> 00:47:37.360
of a literally earthly wealth
and Epicurean unbelief,

00:47:37.360 --> 00:47:39.970
it seeks preemptively
to recruit that future

00:47:39.970 --> 00:47:41.660
for the conservative, religious

00:47:41.660 --> 00:47:44.640
and heroic aims of Tasso's poem.

00:47:44.640 --> 00:47:46.430
But it may succeed in doing so,

00:47:46.430 --> 00:47:50.763
like the conversion of the
magus himself, only in part.

00:47:52.190 --> 00:47:54.670
Now by contrast, Spenser's cave of Mammon

00:47:54.670 --> 00:47:57.000
in book two of canto
seven of The Faerie Queene

00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:01.370
reduces this future to a
bourgeois world of money

00:48:01.370 --> 00:48:05.420
and in doing so, he demonizes
the Scheria Carthage episode.

00:48:05.420 --> 00:48:07.230
Drawing on the same sources

00:48:07.230 --> 00:48:11.550
as Tasso's episode of the magus
and on the episode itself,

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:14.730
Spenser first places the
underground treasure cave of Mammon

00:48:14.730 --> 00:48:16.980
next door to Pluto's hell,

00:48:16.980 --> 00:48:20.970
activating the similarity
of names, excuse me,

00:48:20.970 --> 00:48:24.620
of Pluto and Plutus, the god of wealth.

00:48:24.620 --> 00:48:27.320
And then shows it leading into hell itself

00:48:27.320 --> 00:48:28.600
in the Garden of Proserpina

00:48:28.600 --> 00:48:31.030
and the adjoining River Causidus.

00:48:31.030 --> 00:48:32.710
Covered with smoke and soot,

00:48:32.710 --> 00:48:35.047
Mammon first appears to quote,

00:48:35.047 --> 00:48:39.150
"Have been seared in the
smith's fire-spitting forge."

00:48:39.150 --> 00:48:41.380
And his realm contains a forge.

00:48:41.380 --> 00:48:44.910
Its furnaces and bellows manned by fiends.

00:48:44.910 --> 00:48:46.730
Spenser associates its fires,

00:48:46.730 --> 00:48:49.040
difficult to distinguish from hellfire

00:48:49.040 --> 00:48:50.920
with Vulcan and earlier,

00:48:50.920 --> 00:48:54.130
under his other Virgilian
name with Mulciber.

00:48:54.130 --> 00:48:57.520
This smithy does not seem to produce

00:48:57.520 --> 00:49:00.420
any Hephaestian artwork or weaponry.

00:49:00.420 --> 00:49:03.010
Just the precious metals themselves

00:49:03.010 --> 00:49:06.144
with which the money
god earlier points out,

00:49:06.144 --> 00:49:10.400
Guyon might buy shields, steeds and arms

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:13.080
as well as crowns and kingdoms

00:49:13.080 --> 00:49:17.930
but it may have produced
a Hephaestian automata

00:49:17.930 --> 00:49:21.092
in the allegorical figure of disdain.

00:49:21.092 --> 00:49:24.130
A giant made of gold and 1590,
The Faerie Queene of iron

00:49:24.130 --> 00:49:26.330
in its 1596 version.

00:49:26.330 --> 00:49:30.313
Who dogs Guyon's steps, ready
to snatch and destroy him.

00:49:31.547 --> 00:49:36.280
Guyon replies that he
would rather gain kingdoms

00:49:36.280 --> 00:49:40.630
the old fashioned way, by
the brute force of arms

00:49:40.630 --> 00:49:42.580
and in an appointed aristocratic threat

00:49:42.580 --> 00:49:44.490
against this capitalist upstart

00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:46.440
asserts that he would quote,

00:49:46.440 --> 00:49:49.240
be lured of those that riches have

00:49:49.240 --> 00:49:53.670
than them to have myself
and be their servile slave.

00:49:53.670 --> 00:49:56.200
Mammon looks like a member
of the Fugger family

00:49:56.200 --> 00:49:57.120
and merchant.

00:49:57.120 --> 00:50:00.960
Fugger banking and merchant family

00:50:00.960 --> 00:50:04.480
who monopolized mining
and trade in metals.

00:50:04.480 --> 00:50:07.060
Mammon represents the
new world of capitalism

00:50:07.060 --> 00:50:08.960
and its urban middle class.

00:50:08.960 --> 00:50:11.800
He leads Guyon into a throne room

00:50:11.800 --> 00:50:14.660
that is long and wide as its sum yield

00:50:14.660 --> 00:50:17.400
or solemn temple were.

00:50:17.400 --> 00:50:21.020
That is the haunt of
guildsmen and of lawyers.

00:50:21.020 --> 00:50:23.913
The latter like Ariosto's judge.

00:50:25.340 --> 00:50:28.260
On the throne is his daughter Philotime,

00:50:28.260 --> 00:50:31.590
the lover of honor who fuels ambition

00:50:31.590 --> 00:50:34.180
and is a kind of alternative ego.

00:50:34.180 --> 00:50:35.590
Sorry, excuse me.

00:50:35.590 --> 00:50:39.670
Is a kind of alternative
or allegorical alter ego

00:50:39.670 --> 00:50:41.570
of Queen Elizabeth.

00:50:41.570 --> 00:50:44.660
The bourgeoisie claims
its own independent realm

00:50:44.660 --> 00:50:47.600
within the heroic realm of the poem.

00:50:47.600 --> 00:50:49.170
The episode reveals its links

00:50:49.170 --> 00:50:52.230
to the Phaeacian Carthage
model at the level of plot

00:50:52.230 --> 00:50:54.390
when Mammon, like Alcinous,

00:50:54.390 --> 00:50:58.090
offers his daughter in
marriage to the visiting hero.

00:50:58.090 --> 00:51:00.410
Guyon turns him down with a politeness

00:51:00.410 --> 00:51:02.930
that's bordering on sarcasm.

00:51:02.930 --> 00:51:07.020
It would be too great a
theologically colored grace

00:51:07.020 --> 00:51:08.470
conferred on him he says,

00:51:08.470 --> 00:51:11.160
when of course it would
be he, the gentleman,

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:14.270
who would confer honor on the
moneyed citizen's daughter

00:51:14.270 --> 00:51:15.760
he might marry.

00:51:15.760 --> 00:51:19.160
Perhaps the honor her
name says she craves.

00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:22.400
So many Elizabethan gentlemen did,

00:51:22.400 --> 00:51:26.230
replenishing their own family
fortunes in the process.

00:51:26.230 --> 00:51:30.250
Nor will Guyon eat the
proffered golden fruit,

00:51:30.250 --> 00:51:33.593
nor sit down on Mammon's sliver stool.

00:51:33.593 --> 00:51:35.580
There is not going to be any Phaeacian

00:51:35.580 --> 00:51:38.700
or Carthaginian banquet in this episode.

00:51:38.700 --> 00:51:41.990
Guyon just says no to
all these temptations

00:51:41.990 --> 00:51:43.430
of Spenser's present

00:51:43.430 --> 00:51:46.110
and signals the poem's
aristocratic disdain

00:51:46.110 --> 00:51:48.120
for the new money of the city

00:51:48.120 --> 00:51:50.423
better than any allegorical giant.

00:51:51.460 --> 00:51:55.830
But Mammon spells out
the times have changed.

00:51:55.830 --> 00:51:58.620
How that does live in later times

00:51:58.620 --> 00:52:03.620
must wage thy works for wealth
and life for gold engage.

00:52:04.070 --> 00:52:07.330
He is the capitalist
future that is already here

00:52:07.330 --> 00:52:10.400
and that threatens to
relegate the heroic world

00:52:10.400 --> 00:52:13.360
of Faerieland to the past.

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:15.240
From Mammon's point of view,

00:52:15.240 --> 00:52:17.070
perspective already available

00:52:17.070 --> 00:52:19.680
to Spenser's first and later readers,

00:52:19.680 --> 00:52:24.680
these cracks in epic's aristocratic
facade are easier to see

00:52:25.200 --> 00:52:28.280
and I simply can't really resist this.

00:52:28.280 --> 00:52:32.340
Spenser wickedly parodies
Guyon's intentious disdain

00:52:32.340 --> 00:52:36.190
for Mammon's riches and
reduces it to braggadocio.

00:52:36.190 --> 00:52:38.940
In the world of Braggadocio himself.

00:52:38.940 --> 00:52:41.400
In the ensuing well
back of Hellenore Fablio

00:52:42.370 --> 00:52:44.840
of books three, nine and ten.

00:52:44.840 --> 00:52:48.070
When Malbecco, miser and doublet of Mammon

00:52:48.070 --> 00:52:51.144
offers the imposter knight money

00:52:51.144 --> 00:52:53.223
in order to enlist him in the recovery

00:52:53.223 --> 00:52:57.543
of his runaway wife,
Hellenore, Braggadocio replies.

00:52:58.778 --> 00:53:03.778
"I loathe as dung, Nadine my due reward.

00:53:04.471 --> 00:53:07.517
"Fame is my need and glory, virtuous pray.

00:53:07.517 --> 00:53:10.127
"But minds of mortal men are much marred

00:53:10.127 --> 00:53:14.460
"and moved amidst with nasty
mucks and neat regard."

00:53:14.460 --> 00:53:17.480
The over the top alliteration
of the final couplet

00:53:17.480 --> 00:53:19.880
gives the game away and
lowers still further,

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:22.400
the low comedy of the episode.

00:53:22.400 --> 00:53:25.486
Braggadocio goes on to
steal Malbecco's gold

00:53:25.486 --> 00:53:28.310
as Paridell has stolen and abandoned

00:53:28.310 --> 00:53:30.400
Malbecco's wife Hellenore

00:53:30.400 --> 00:53:31.470
and these false knights

00:53:31.470 --> 00:53:34.990
just might disclose the
true nature of knighthood.

00:53:34.990 --> 00:53:36.610
The episode further rewrites

00:53:36.610 --> 00:53:39.780
the earlier Mammon
allegory to another version

00:53:39.780 --> 00:53:42.590
of the Phaeacian Carthaginian banquet.

00:53:42.590 --> 00:53:44.960
The meal that the unwilling host Malbecco

00:53:44.960 --> 00:53:47.280
is forced to offer the wandering knights

00:53:47.280 --> 00:53:50.760
who otherwise threaten
to burn down his castle.

00:53:50.760 --> 00:53:52.460
And at which Paridell and Britomart

00:53:52.460 --> 00:53:55.010
each recount their Trojan genealogies

00:53:55.010 --> 00:53:58.880
in the manner of the
guests Odysseus and Aeneas.

00:53:58.880 --> 00:54:01.580
Eventually Hellenore herself
sets Malbecco's house

00:54:01.580 --> 00:54:04.750
in riches of fire like
another burning Troy,

00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:06.900
as Spenser offers a debased version

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:09.053
of the epic myth of Troy.

00:54:10.564 --> 00:54:13.270
In a May December story of adultery

00:54:13.270 --> 00:54:15.100
out of Chaucer's Merchant's Tale

00:54:15.100 --> 00:54:17.480
and Gascoigne's The Adventures of F.J.,

00:54:17.480 --> 00:54:19.700
novellas like the ones told

00:54:19.700 --> 00:54:21.670
at the end of the Orlando Furioso.

00:54:22.720 --> 00:54:25.300
Or he reduces that myth, the Trojan myth

00:54:25.300 --> 00:54:28.750
as the Phaeacian bard
Demodocus does in The Odyssey.

00:54:28.750 --> 00:54:31.580
To the adultery of an
Aphrodite and an Ares

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:35.300
at the expense of a gold
handling smith Hephaestus

00:54:35.300 --> 00:54:38.250
with little of the Olympian
Phaeacian sophistication

00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:39.880
and good cheer.

00:54:39.880 --> 00:54:41.950
Instead, Spenser risks

00:54:41.950 --> 00:54:45.961
having the national dynastic
project Troy-novant,

00:54:45.961 --> 00:54:47.300
Gloriana, The Faerie Queene.

00:54:47.300 --> 00:54:50.190
The epicness of his poem swallowed up

00:54:50.190 --> 00:54:52.310
into the moneyed modern world

00:54:52.310 --> 00:54:54.980
and its lower literary genres.

00:54:54.980 --> 00:54:57.630
Malbecco turns into jealousy,

00:54:57.630 --> 00:55:00.203
the ethos particular to the novel.

00:55:01.422 --> 00:55:05.730
Hellenore, Helen-or ends
up among the satyrs.

00:55:05.730 --> 00:55:07.513
That is in satire.

00:55:12.080 --> 00:55:15.500
Milton's Eden is the
Scheria of Paradise Lost

00:55:15.500 --> 00:55:19.663
while Pandemonium is the poem's
malsabarian Vulcanian Rome.

00:55:20.500 --> 00:55:22.980
Paradise Lost starts at
the beginning of time

00:55:22.980 --> 00:55:25.760
and treats events that
shape us as human beings.

00:55:25.760 --> 00:55:28.430
It cannot but look forward, Hegel notes,

00:55:28.430 --> 00:55:31.860
in order to disqualify
Paradise Lost as an epic.

00:55:31.860 --> 00:55:34.947
He says, "In Milton, we always
find feeling in consideration

00:55:34.947 --> 00:55:36.767
"drawn from a modern imagination,

00:55:36.767 --> 00:55:39.660
"the moral ideas of his day."

00:55:39.660 --> 00:55:41.590
This modernity takes two forms.

00:55:41.590 --> 00:55:43.820
Utopian and the Scheria-like Eden

00:55:43.820 --> 00:55:46.600
which is not primitive enough for Hegel,

00:55:46.600 --> 00:55:51.110
demonic in Satan's Hephaestian
capital and seat of monarchy.

00:55:51.110 --> 00:55:55.763
And once, too atavistic and
all too actual for Milton.

00:55:57.320 --> 00:55:58.610
Milton gives us the
epic coordinates of Eden

00:55:58.610 --> 00:56:01.690
in the menu that Eve gathers together

00:56:01.690 --> 00:56:03.350
for the couple's first dinner party

00:56:03.350 --> 00:56:05.430
in honor of the Angel Raphael,

00:56:05.430 --> 00:56:08.423
the poem's version of the epic banquet.

00:56:09.400 --> 00:56:12.380
Whatever earth, all-bearing
mother yields in India,

00:56:12.380 --> 00:56:14.970
east or west or middle shore in Pontus

00:56:14.970 --> 00:56:18.920
or the Punic Coast or
where Alcinous reigned,

00:56:18.920 --> 00:56:20.970
fruit of all kinds.

00:56:20.970 --> 00:56:23.430
The whole globe has contracted into Eden

00:56:23.430 --> 00:56:25.250
which buoys between east and west,

00:56:25.250 --> 00:56:28.130
takes on the milieu of Mediterranean epic.

00:56:28.130 --> 00:56:31.940
Of Pontic, Colchis of the
Argonautica of Valerius Flaccus

00:56:31.940 --> 00:56:36.080
of Punic Carthage and of
Scheria, the realm of Alcinous.

00:56:36.080 --> 00:56:38.470
The foodstuffs announce the familiar topas

00:56:38.470 --> 00:56:41.920
of the hospitable meal
accompanied by storytelling.

00:56:41.920 --> 00:56:44.330
What follows Raphael's too long narrations

00:56:44.330 --> 00:56:46.067
of the war in heaven

00:56:46.067 --> 00:56:47.400
and the creation of God's new universe

00:56:47.400 --> 00:56:50.860
correspond to the songs
of Demodocus and Yopus,

00:56:50.860 --> 00:56:53.930
told here in a reversal of epic convention

00:56:53.930 --> 00:56:57.170
by the guest rather than the host.

00:56:57.170 --> 00:57:01.563
The Phaeacian model remains
in place in the poem.

00:57:03.095 --> 00:57:06.480
Satan's subsequent approach
to Eve harkens back

00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:09.810
to Odysseus' first
encounter with Nausicaa.

00:57:09.810 --> 00:57:13.890
It casts Scheria itself as
a place of idenic innocence.

00:57:13.890 --> 00:57:15.870
Pious though they are,

00:57:15.870 --> 00:57:18.880
Adam and Eve lived the Epicurean good life

00:57:18.880 --> 00:57:20.410
of the Phaeacians.

00:57:20.410 --> 00:57:21.930
UnwarliKe,

00:57:21.930 --> 00:57:22.840
looking forward to the nightly lovemaking

00:57:22.840 --> 00:57:24.420
that caps their existence.

00:57:24.420 --> 00:57:27.100
Then listen with fascination
to Raphael's narration

00:57:27.100 --> 00:57:30.050
of the war in heaven that
casts such epic heroism

00:57:30.050 --> 00:57:33.410
into a quaint literary
past made ridiculous

00:57:33.410 --> 00:57:35.593
by Satan's invention of artillery.

00:57:36.539 --> 00:57:38.675
Yes artillery was there
from the beginning,

00:57:38.675 --> 00:57:40.260
at least from the second
day of the heavenly battle

00:57:40.260 --> 00:57:43.203
to ruin the individual heroics of epic.

00:57:44.898 --> 00:57:47.240
Eden is also a realm of
scientific enlightenment

00:57:47.240 --> 00:57:49.230
where Adam and Raphael can engage

00:57:49.230 --> 00:57:53.640
in astronomical observation,
much like Tasso's Magus.

00:57:53.640 --> 00:57:55.780
Yopus is here too.

00:57:55.780 --> 00:57:56.980
Adam and Eve thus live

00:57:56.980 --> 00:57:59.130
not only at the beginning
of human existence

00:57:59.130 --> 00:58:02.220
but in a post-futile, post-epic age.

00:58:02.220 --> 00:58:05.657
As Fredric Jameson boldly
and brilliantly puts it,

00:58:05.657 --> 00:58:08.407
"Alongside the futile
world of God and His court,

00:58:08.407 --> 00:58:10.677
"of Satan and his host,

00:58:10.677 --> 00:58:13.603
"Adam is clearly of another
species, the commoner.

00:58:14.579 --> 00:58:15.412
"The first bourgeois.

00:58:15.412 --> 00:58:18.907
"That extraordinary mutation
which is middle class man,

00:58:18.907 --> 00:58:22.835
"destined as we know today to be fruitful

00:58:22.835 --> 00:58:24.985
"and to multiply and
to inherit the earth."

00:58:25.997 --> 00:58:26.830
Unquote.

00:58:27.895 --> 00:58:29.043
Struggle with quoting Jameson is the,

00:58:30.229 --> 00:58:33.810
what surrounds it does not seem so good.

00:58:33.810 --> 00:58:37.610
Satan first enters Eden
in simile as a thief,

00:58:37.610 --> 00:58:42.150
then to unhoard the cash
of some rich burger.

00:58:42.150 --> 00:58:44.390
Eden, like other epic versions

00:58:44.390 --> 00:58:47.440
of Phaeacian Scheria
is a utopian projection

00:58:47.440 --> 00:58:48.860
of a modern world

00:58:48.860 --> 00:58:52.800
that has superseded
epic's own heroic realm.

00:58:52.800 --> 00:58:54.540
The realm of war.

00:58:54.540 --> 00:58:58.030
It is Milton's dream of
a virtuous bourgeoisie,

00:58:58.030 --> 00:59:00.980
a bourgeoisie still unfallen

00:59:00.980 --> 00:59:03.990
and thus not yet subject
to the sardonic tinge

00:59:03.990 --> 00:59:06.430
of Jameson's description.

00:59:06.430 --> 00:59:09.090
In a significant revision of his models,

00:59:09.090 --> 00:59:11.280
Milton makes this Phaeacian Eden

00:59:11.280 --> 00:59:16.100
not the region of unbelief
but of true individual faith.

00:59:16.100 --> 00:59:18.550
The more so when it becomes after the fall

00:59:18.550 --> 00:59:20.970
of spiritual paradise within

00:59:20.970 --> 00:59:24.160
as opposed to the worldly
religion of the state

00:59:24.160 --> 00:59:26.870
and its enforcers of conscience.

00:59:26.870 --> 00:59:29.760
For at the same time that
God makes another heaven

00:59:29.760 --> 00:59:31.620
for these new creatures,

00:59:31.620 --> 00:59:35.220
Satan and his followers,
Mammon and Mulciber

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:37.110
make their own imitation of heaven

00:59:37.110 --> 00:59:40.350
in their infernal capital
city of Pandemonium.

00:59:40.350 --> 00:59:44.130
They do so slightly after
God has made the new Eden

00:59:44.130 --> 00:59:47.270
and theirs too is a version of modernity.

00:59:47.270 --> 00:59:49.870
The first prototype of the seats of rising

00:59:49.870 --> 00:59:53.400
and falling empires that
waltzed through the globe,

00:59:53.400 --> 00:59:55.730
cataloged in book 11.

00:59:55.730 --> 00:59:57.020
Borrowed from Spenser,

00:59:57.020 --> 00:59:59.810
Miton's fallen angel Mammon mines gold

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:01.543
from the bowels of hell.

01:00:02.639 --> 01:00:06.889
Mulciber's Hephaestus,
Mulciber, Mulphaestus sorry.

01:00:07.750 --> 01:00:10.010
Formerly the architect in heaven

01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:13.650
works the metal into molds
and builds the palace.

01:00:13.650 --> 01:00:16.720
Pandemonium resembles both Dido's Carthage

01:00:16.720 --> 01:00:19.940
and its wealth and
built in a day as it is,

01:00:19.940 --> 01:00:23.113
the military Rome that was
Carthage's mortal enemy.

01:00:24.214 --> 01:00:25.370
The conflation suggests the devils

01:00:25.370 --> 01:00:27.000
are their own worst enemies

01:00:27.000 --> 01:00:30.690
and that this seat of empire
too is doomed to fail.

01:00:30.690 --> 01:00:34.130
Such capital cities, Hugh
Trevor-Roper has argued,

01:00:34.130 --> 01:00:37.630
by the unsustainable consumption
of their royal courts

01:00:37.630 --> 01:00:40.910
and by the expense of their
military establishments

01:00:40.910 --> 01:00:43.010
had doomed the mercantile city states

01:00:43.010 --> 01:00:45.490
which had been the
cradle of the Renaissance

01:00:45.490 --> 01:00:48.080
and its humanism and
led to the revolutions

01:00:48.080 --> 01:00:49.833
of the mid 17th century.

01:00:50.919 --> 01:00:53.870
The English Revolution
most memorable among them.

01:00:53.870 --> 01:00:56.750
In fact, as shown in the
debate in the infernal Council

01:00:56.750 --> 01:00:59.650
in book one, Mammon, the representative

01:00:59.650 --> 01:01:03.715
of the city bourgeoisie,
hater of royal pomp

01:01:03.715 --> 01:01:07.220
and advocate of peace would
like to be left alone to quote,

01:01:07.220 --> 01:01:09.033
live to ourselves.

01:01:09.976 --> 01:01:13.723
Though in this vast recess,
free and to none accountable.

01:01:14.620 --> 01:01:16.750
However, Hawksby points
out that Mammon represents

01:01:16.750 --> 01:01:19.190
a double of the middle class Adam,

01:01:19.190 --> 01:01:22.440
but a godless demonic
one and he is swept up,

01:01:22.440 --> 01:01:23.790
willing or not.

01:01:23.790 --> 01:01:26.600
Not only into the building of pandemonium

01:01:26.600 --> 01:01:30.023
but into Satan's project
of war and imperialism.

01:01:31.339 --> 01:01:34.280
Mulciber reappears in
the visions of the future

01:01:34.280 --> 01:01:36.370
that Michael bestows on Adam,

01:01:36.370 --> 01:01:38.870
themselves modeled on
Vulcan's shield of Aeneas

01:01:38.870 --> 01:01:41.670
in the Aeneid and Hephaestus'
shield of Achilles

01:01:41.670 --> 01:01:43.320
in the Iliad.

01:01:43.320 --> 01:01:44.510
In the third vision,

01:01:44.510 --> 01:01:48.833
the unnamed Tubalcain whose
name was traditionally seen

01:01:48.833 --> 01:01:53.451
to be a variant of Vulcain
invents metalworking.

01:01:53.451 --> 01:01:55.460
The daughters of this race of Cain

01:01:55.460 --> 01:01:57.550
will seduce the children of Seth,

01:01:57.550 --> 01:01:59.950
hitherto peaceable and free.

01:01:59.950 --> 01:02:03.760
Quote, "In the amorous net, fast-caught,

01:02:03.760 --> 01:02:06.517
"they liked and each his liking chose."

01:02:06.517 --> 01:02:07.516
Unquote.

01:02:07.516 --> 01:02:09.340
As Milton blends the myth of Pandora,

01:02:09.340 --> 01:02:13.130
originally an automaton
created by Hephaestus Vulcan,

01:02:13.130 --> 01:02:17.610
with the other Hephaestian
story of Ares and Aphrodite.

01:02:17.610 --> 01:02:20.040
The offspring of these ill-mated marriages

01:02:20.040 --> 01:02:21.540
will be the warrior giants,

01:02:21.540 --> 01:02:24.200
the first epic heroes
and empirical conquerors

01:02:25.139 --> 01:02:27.300
in human history and the once godly,

01:02:27.300 --> 01:02:30.503
now made Mammons will
fight at possible quote,

01:02:31.395 --> 01:02:34.317
"To live secure, worldly or dissolute

01:02:34.317 --> 01:02:37.717
"on what their Lord shall
leave them to enjoy."

01:02:38.758 --> 01:02:39.720
Unquote.

01:02:39.720 --> 01:02:44.460
Paradise Lost offers two
versions of the bourgeois future.

01:02:44.460 --> 01:02:47.396
One, Phaeacian and utopian

01:02:47.396 --> 01:02:50.050
that appears to supersede
the royal aristocratic order

01:02:50.050 --> 01:02:52.000
and its epic fictions

01:02:52.000 --> 01:02:54.550
and one in which that
regressive warrior order

01:02:54.550 --> 01:02:59.410
keeps returning as it had in
the restoration in England

01:02:59.410 --> 01:03:01.950
and at which a moneyed
middle class finds itself

01:03:01.950 --> 01:03:05.060
as the beneficiary and silent accomplice

01:03:05.060 --> 01:03:08.830
of a satanic military
industrial establishment.

01:03:08.830 --> 01:03:12.400
In the second version, epic
and its glorification of war

01:03:12.400 --> 01:03:15.810
never dies away, however
much Paradise Lost

01:03:15.810 --> 01:03:17.903
may claim to end the genre.

01:03:19.313 --> 01:03:22.240
We can ask which of
these futures is our own

01:03:22.240 --> 01:03:23.760
and let me just conclude.

01:03:23.760 --> 01:03:25.600
We can pose this question here

01:03:25.600 --> 01:03:27.680
and in other epics I have discussed

01:03:27.680 --> 01:03:30.137
because the epic world is not quote,

01:03:30.137 --> 01:03:32.007
"An entirely finished thing,

01:03:32.007 --> 01:03:34.717
"not only as an authentic
event of the distant past

01:03:34.717 --> 01:03:37.690
"but also on its own terms
and by its own standards."

01:03:37.690 --> 01:03:38.523
Unquote.

01:03:38.523 --> 01:03:39.667
It is not quote,

01:03:39.667 --> 01:03:43.047
"Impossible to change,
to rethink or reevaluate

01:03:43.047 --> 01:03:44.180
"anything in it."

01:03:44.180 --> 01:03:45.470
Unquote.

01:03:45.470 --> 01:03:47.240
These words are Bakhtin's

01:03:47.240 --> 01:03:50.470
and they're a doubling down on
Hegel that is influenced too

01:03:50.470 --> 01:03:53.800
by Lukacs' notion of the
roundedness of the epic

01:03:53.800 --> 01:03:56.030
that is the Homeric world.

01:03:56.030 --> 01:03:59.660
Unlike Lukacs and Hegel,
Bakhtin does not romanticize

01:03:59.660 --> 01:04:01.460
this epic world as a place

01:04:01.460 --> 01:04:04.160
where one could be at home everywhere.

01:04:04.160 --> 01:04:06.960
The land of unified consciousness.

01:04:06.960 --> 01:04:10.710
There is nothing living
about this world at all

01:04:10.710 --> 01:04:13.154
and more than the Odyssey does

01:04:13.154 --> 01:04:14.572
in the underworld of book 11,

01:04:14.572 --> 01:04:16.770
Bakhtin consigns the genre to a past

01:04:16.770 --> 01:04:19.533
that is not only absolute but dead.

01:04:20.400 --> 01:04:22.510
Bakhtin broadly assimilates in epic,

01:04:22.510 --> 01:04:25.310
the other high genres
of classical antiquity

01:04:25.310 --> 01:04:26.440
and the Middle Ages.

01:04:26.440 --> 01:04:30.060
All of them to be made
obsolete by the novel.

01:04:30.060 --> 01:04:32.830
His claim is transparently false

01:04:32.830 --> 01:04:35.680
as if any work of literature
could wall itself off

01:04:35.680 --> 01:04:37.855
from its readers and quote,

01:04:37.855 --> 01:04:40.605
"Not commit an individual
point of view or evaluation."

01:04:41.454 --> 01:04:42.458
Unquote.

01:04:42.458 --> 01:04:46.333
It's based on a mistaken
conception, excuse me.

01:04:47.332 --> 01:04:49.383
Of the, excuse me.

01:04:50.411 --> 01:04:52.320
It is based on a mistaken conception

01:04:52.320 --> 01:04:55.554
of the suppression of the
heroic that we've built in,

01:04:55.554 --> 01:04:58.487
we've seen epic built into its fictions.

01:04:58.487 --> 01:05:01.227
"Both Stinger and the listener,

01:05:01.227 --> 01:05:04.117
"imminent in the epic
genre," Bakhtin writes,

01:05:04.117 --> 01:05:05.727
"are located at the same time

01:05:05.727 --> 01:05:08.017
"and on the same evaluative plane

01:05:08.017 --> 01:05:10.137
"but the represented
world of the hero stands

01:05:10.137 --> 01:05:12.934
"on an ultimately
different and inaccessible

01:05:12.934 --> 01:05:16.327
"time and value plane
separated by epic distance.

01:05:16.327 --> 01:05:20.070
"The space between them is
filled with national tradition."

01:05:20.070 --> 01:05:21.330
Unquote.

01:05:21.330 --> 01:05:24.140
Such tradition we are asked
to believe freezes the meaning

01:05:24.140 --> 01:05:25.110
of the epic world

01:05:25.110 --> 01:05:28.030
and the deeds done within
it as something sacrosanct,

01:05:28.030 --> 01:05:30.640
beyond criticism and evaluation.

01:05:30.640 --> 01:05:33.703
But tradition does not
do anything of the sort.

01:05:35.559 --> 01:05:39.530
Sacred books and just as much,

01:05:39.530 --> 01:05:41.390
perhaps even more than great literature

01:05:41.390 --> 01:05:44.093
which sometimes acquires something

01:05:44.093 --> 01:05:47.090
of the aura of the
sacred are reinterpreted,

01:05:47.090 --> 01:05:50.210
argued over, reattached to the present

01:05:50.210 --> 01:05:54.230
precisely because they have
something at stake in them.

01:05:54.230 --> 01:05:57.170
If one wishes to grant him
the benefit of the doubt,

01:05:57.170 --> 01:05:58.840
and I'm not so inclined.

01:05:58.840 --> 01:06:00.360
Bakhtin may be isolating

01:06:00.360 --> 01:06:03.757
a particularly reverential
mode of reading conservative,

01:06:03.757 --> 01:06:05.370
of reading sorry.

01:06:05.370 --> 01:06:08.640
That is conservative and
class-bound in nature

01:06:08.640 --> 01:06:11.180
versus the more open, democratic ethos

01:06:11.180 --> 01:06:13.530
that he ascribes to the novel.

01:06:13.530 --> 01:06:15.650
But epic from its Homeric beginnings

01:06:15.650 --> 01:06:19.840
has figured a post-heroic
audience inside the poems.

01:06:19.840 --> 01:06:22.270
In dialogue with the heroes themselves,

01:06:22.270 --> 01:06:24.890
opening up a portal to the future.

01:06:24.890 --> 01:06:26.980
Starting with Virgil and taken up

01:06:26.980 --> 01:06:28.710
by the Renaissance epic poets,

01:06:28.710 --> 01:06:31.730
these moments of contact
with a more modern future,

01:06:31.730 --> 01:06:34.150
especially with more modern readers

01:06:34.150 --> 01:06:36.540
measure the, excuse me.

01:06:36.540 --> 01:06:39.394
Measure the lateness of the poem

01:06:39.394 --> 01:06:40.733
to the tradition of the genre

01:06:40.733 --> 01:06:42.780
and to its celebration of martial prowess.

01:06:42.780 --> 01:06:45.120
In all of these cases I have suggested,

01:06:45.120 --> 01:06:48.720
this modernity is linked to
material wealth and technology.

01:06:48.720 --> 01:06:52.230
To alternative ways of
producing human prosperity

01:06:52.230 --> 01:06:57.230
that do not depend or do not
seem to depend on warfare.

01:06:58.180 --> 01:07:00.470
This modernity looks
back into the epic world

01:07:00.470 --> 01:07:02.240
and asks awkward questions

01:07:02.240 --> 01:07:05.090
about the purposes of heroism itself.

01:07:05.090 --> 01:07:07.580
About the greed that is taken for granted

01:07:07.580 --> 01:07:10.110
by a belligerent, feudal society.

01:07:10.110 --> 01:07:12.890
At the same time proposes enlightened,

01:07:12.890 --> 01:07:15.180
scientific attitudes that question

01:07:15.180 --> 01:07:18.230
the religious props of that society.

01:07:18.230 --> 01:07:21.290
Through its glimpses into a
future dwelling side by side

01:07:21.290 --> 01:07:23.640
with the present of the world of heroes,

01:07:23.640 --> 01:07:27.060
epic, far from being sacrosanct depicts

01:07:27.060 --> 01:07:29.067
its own desacralization.

01:07:30.054 --> 01:07:30.887
Thank you.

01:07:32.232 --> 01:07:35.232
(audience applauds)

