in the audience of Demodocus'
song might be seeing

01:11:42.252 --> 01:11:45.890
that Hephaestus gets laughed
at by the other gods,

01:11:45.890 --> 01:11:47.720
might be seeing that there's no mention

01:11:47.720 --> 01:11:51.000
that he receives a financial recompense

01:11:51.000 --> 01:11:52.640
and we conclude the song

01:11:52.640 --> 01:11:55.483
just looking at Aphrodite taking a bath.

01:11:56.547 --> 01:11:57.830
Like the gods who are looking at her

01:11:57.830 --> 01:11:59.420
getting caught in the net

01:11:59.420 --> 01:12:03.300
and so I'm just wondering
if even on the level

01:12:03.300 --> 01:12:05.249
of the internal audience,

01:12:05.249 --> 01:12:06.385
Odysseus himself,

01:12:06.385 --> 01:12:08.140
there's a way in which
this portrait of modernity

01:12:08.140 --> 01:12:10.120
is pushing Odysseus back,

01:12:10.120 --> 01:12:12.960
posing a warning that
legal bonds aren't enough

01:12:12.960 --> 01:12:16.410
and he has to kill the suitors to preserve

01:12:16.410 --> 01:12:17.873
any sense of dignity.

01:12:20.104 --> 01:12:24.620
- Well I, the poem clearly takes the,

01:12:24.620 --> 01:12:27.440
is on the side of Odysseus.

01:12:27.440 --> 01:12:30.250
So when he comes back and kills everybody,

01:12:30.250 --> 01:12:33.580
the poem is on his side.

01:12:33.580 --> 01:12:35.970
However, one can ask oneself

01:12:35.970 --> 01:12:39.480
whether the story of Ares and Aphrodite

01:12:39.480 --> 01:12:42.200
does not offer a different way

01:12:42.200 --> 01:12:45.450
that these things could
happen and that you know,

01:12:45.450 --> 01:12:50.450
you know if you decide that
you wanted to absolutely,

01:12:51.830 --> 01:12:53.421
that you have to kill people

01:12:53.421 --> 01:12:56.030
and use violence at the end,

01:12:56.030 --> 01:12:59.940
then you can make these
objections to the story and say,

01:12:59.940 --> 01:13:03.120
well does, how well does
Hephaestus really come out

01:13:03.120 --> 01:13:04.600
at the end of this?

01:13:04.600 --> 01:13:07.992
But there does seem to
be some sense that okay,

01:13:07.992 --> 01:13:11.663
what more did you expect
from Aphrodite anyway?

01:13:12.640 --> 01:13:17.470
And that, but also it's
a comic song for it.

01:13:17.470 --> 01:13:19.340
For the Phaeacians.

01:13:20.568 --> 01:13:23.050
Whatever it is for Odysseus
may be something else

01:13:23.050 --> 01:13:25.305
but as far as the
Phaeacians are concerned,

01:13:25.305 --> 01:13:26.138
I think that they,

01:13:26.138 --> 01:13:27.380
they're laughing along with the gods

01:13:27.380 --> 01:13:29.508
and they're laughing along, you know,

01:13:29.508 --> 01:13:34.508
the dirty joke of Hermes and Apollo.

01:13:35.168 --> 01:13:37.197
Hermes says, "Hey, tie me up, tie me up.

01:13:37.197 --> 01:13:39.787
"You know as long as you
tie me up with Aphrodite,

01:13:39.787 --> 01:13:41.520
"I'm fine."

01:13:41.520 --> 01:13:43.830
And I think we're supposed to feel

01:13:43.830 --> 01:13:47.560
that that's their kind of song

01:13:47.560 --> 01:13:50.090
as opposed to the two epic things

01:13:50.090 --> 01:13:51.590
that are on either side of it.

01:13:52.925 --> 01:13:55.793
But that's, that's my line.

01:13:57.667 --> 01:13:59.044
I guess.

01:13:59.044 --> 01:14:00.210
But it's a great question and it's also,

01:14:00.210 --> 01:14:02.700
I mean you're right.

01:14:02.700 --> 01:14:07.304
The poem, the poem finally
takes the side of Odysseus.

01:14:07.304 --> 01:14:08.221
That's not.

01:14:21.600 --> 01:14:24.948
- [Woman] Hello, thank you
this is really interesting

01:14:24.948 --> 01:14:26.950
even though I had to
come in slightly late.

01:14:26.950 --> 01:14:30.792
But I was wondering if
you were going to extend

01:14:30.792 --> 01:14:32.930
what you're doing beyond Milton,

01:14:32.930 --> 01:14:35.660
if there was another epic beyond that

01:14:35.660 --> 01:14:37.410
that you would like to fit in

01:14:37.410 --> 01:14:39.110
to what you've been talking about.

01:14:41.932 --> 01:14:44.090
- I mean I think alas,

01:14:44.090 --> 01:14:48.750
that Milton really does
slightly close the tradition.

01:14:48.750 --> 01:14:53.750
There is Lucrezia Marinella's
terrible poem the "Enrico".

01:14:55.864 --> 01:15:00.083
Which is, it's actually pre-Milton.

01:15:02.242 --> 01:15:05.620
Does have an interesting moment

01:15:05.620 --> 01:15:08.210
where of a character named Cercina.

01:15:09.089 --> 01:15:13.310
So little Cerci who is a
kind of natural scientist

01:15:13.310 --> 01:15:18.180
and where some of these same
topoi are being redeployed

01:15:19.090 --> 01:15:22.457
and you know, but it's a
kind of footnote I think.

01:15:22.457 --> 01:15:26.694
I'm not really sure where
one goes after Milton.

01:15:26.694 --> 01:15:29.690
We really do get the novel
and I'm not sure that there's,

01:15:29.690 --> 01:15:33.230
that this particular topos.
- Yeah.

01:15:33.230 --> 01:15:35.510
- That I can trace much beyond it.

01:15:35.510 --> 01:15:38.060
- [Woman] I mean I haven't
looked into it in any depth

01:15:38.060 --> 01:15:41.650
but I was thinking of Omeros
by Derek Walcott perhaps?

01:15:41.650 --> 01:15:44.257
- Well yeah, I mean there is not.

01:15:44.257 --> 01:15:46.814
I don't think we have.

01:15:46.814 --> 01:15:49.690
I mean, I like Omeros.

01:15:49.690 --> 01:15:54.040
I don't think that there
is a scene that gives us

01:15:54.040 --> 01:15:57.037
this kind of correspondence.
- Yeah, yeah, okay.

01:15:57.037 --> 01:15:59.590
- I mean certainly Omeros
is a meditation on,

01:15:59.590 --> 01:16:02.920
particularly on the
relationship of novel and epic

01:16:02.920 --> 01:16:06.680
and about what happens in a modern,

01:16:06.680 --> 01:16:08.240
the modern commercial world

01:16:09.090 --> 01:16:12.715
and whether there is any real epic left.

01:16:12.715 --> 01:16:16.920
That certainly is part
of its pathos I think.

01:16:16.920 --> 01:16:18.763
- [Woman] Yeah okay, thank you.

01:16:24.490 --> 01:16:25.323
- Chris.

01:16:31.570 --> 01:16:32.830
- [Chris] I know as in a courtroom,

01:16:32.830 --> 01:16:34.250
you're never supposed to ask a question

01:16:34.250 --> 01:16:35.650
you don't know the answer to

01:16:36.491 --> 01:16:38.733
and I really don't know the answer to this

01:16:38.733 --> 01:16:39.566
but I'm gonna ask it anyway.

01:16:39.566 --> 01:16:43.820
What does Veggio's 13th book do

01:16:45.184 --> 01:16:50.081
to the Aeneid as an act of reception

01:16:50.081 --> 01:16:53.273
in your terms?

01:16:58.610 --> 01:17:00.826
Is it fun to think about?

01:17:00.826 --> 01:17:02.310
- Okay, well I mean,

01:17:02.310 --> 01:17:03.700
one of the things,

01:17:03.700 --> 01:17:08.600
clearly part of what that
book's trying to do is to dispel

01:17:08.600 --> 01:17:12.320
the idea that the Aeneid closes as it does

01:17:12.320 --> 01:17:15.720
with a brutal act of violence and revenge

01:17:15.720 --> 01:17:20.720
but rather ends with
marriage and a kind of,

01:17:20.783 --> 01:17:23.043
a kind of happy futurity.

01:17:27.518 --> 01:17:28.351
What it does, it also,

01:17:28.351 --> 01:17:33.330
it may say something
about Veggio's own time.

01:17:37.250 --> 01:17:42.250
Wanting, I'm not sure I can
speculate much further than that

01:17:46.186 --> 01:17:48.003
but it's clearly, it's part of.

01:17:48.003 --> 01:17:51.830
Its sense of wanting to
get Virgil off the hook

01:17:51.830 --> 01:17:55.800
in some ways is part of
what it's responding to.

01:17:55.800 --> 01:17:56.786
But it's also,

01:17:56.786 --> 01:17:57.804
and it's also trying to normal.

01:17:57.804 --> 01:17:58.637
It's trying to is yeah,

01:17:58.637 --> 01:18:00.373
this is what a comic poem at the end.

01:18:01.230 --> 01:18:03.080
It's to take the, one way to look at it

01:18:03.080 --> 01:18:05.420
is to take the Odyssean structure

01:18:05.420 --> 01:18:08.090
of the second part of the Aeneid which,

01:18:08.090 --> 01:18:12.500
and to say yes it really
is all about returning home

01:18:12.500 --> 01:18:14.623
and getting, and unifying.

01:18:15.600 --> 01:18:19.830
Not reuniting but finding a wife.

01:18:19.830 --> 01:18:21.968
And that that,

01:18:21.968 --> 01:18:24.630
I mean that is after all
part of what is happening

01:18:24.630 --> 01:18:27.243
in the second part of the poem.

01:18:28.624 --> 01:18:30.130
And here it just, it's simply.

01:18:30.130 --> 01:18:31.883
Let's take the happy sequel.

01:18:33.044 --> 01:18:34.870
I mean one could think about, anyway.

01:18:36.380 --> 01:18:38.390
I'm trying to, what's
the novel that's based on

01:18:38.390 --> 01:18:39.613
Lavinia's point of view?

01:18:40.600 --> 01:18:42.130
But it is that.
- Lavinia.

01:18:42.130 --> 01:18:42.963
- Yeah, Lavinia.

01:18:43.987 --> 01:18:48.068
It's something like that,

01:18:48.068 --> 01:18:52.247
of trying to make the
Aeneid more palatable

01:18:54.070 --> 01:18:59.070
for the schoolboy audiences
to whom one assumes.

01:19:00.688 --> 01:19:02.780
- [Chris] But not to modernize it.

01:19:02.780 --> 01:19:04.543
- Well it does, I guess.

01:19:05.700 --> 01:19:07.210
I mean yeah, I.

01:19:07.210 --> 01:19:10.190
I don't remember it quite well enough

01:19:10.190 --> 01:19:11.890
to be able to give you the answer.

01:19:15.380 --> 01:19:16.213
You know.

01:19:20.851 --> 01:19:21.720
- [Man] Sorry.

01:19:21.720 --> 01:19:23.910
- [Man] David, I was
thinking of Epic and Empire

01:19:23.910 --> 01:19:25.220
quite a lot as I was sitting here

01:19:25.220 --> 01:19:27.920
because I used to teach in the course,

01:19:27.920 --> 01:19:30.449
I think it's now called English 130

01:19:30.449 --> 01:19:31.282
that used to be called English 129

01:19:31.282 --> 01:19:34.900
and read Epic and Empire to
help me understand the books

01:19:34.900 --> 01:19:37.680
and I guess many of the
themes from Epic and Empire

01:19:37.680 --> 01:19:39.788
sort of underlie what you said today

01:19:39.788 --> 01:19:42.120
but I wonder if looking back you know,

01:19:42.120 --> 01:19:44.700
is there anything you
think differently about

01:19:44.700 --> 01:19:45.690
than when you wrote that?

01:19:45.690 --> 01:19:49.323
Or has this cast any new light
on the work you did then?

01:19:50.413 --> 01:19:51.412
- Well thank you for that.

01:19:51.412 --> 01:19:52.410
Because I mean yeah,

01:19:52.410 --> 01:19:55.780
what I might have said in
my introductory remarks

01:19:55.780 --> 01:19:59.750
to this already overly long lecture.

01:19:59.750 --> 01:20:02.520
Was that this is, it is a kind of rethink.

01:20:02.520 --> 01:20:06.009
I mean it's trying to look at epic

01:20:06.009 --> 01:20:08.460
from a slightly different
point of view than epic,

01:20:09.706 --> 01:20:12.580
than the essay that begins Epic and Empire

01:20:12.580 --> 01:20:16.332
which was again one of
these big overview essays.

01:20:16.332 --> 01:20:19.760
And where I mean I do think,

01:20:19.760 --> 01:20:24.760
I mean epic is clearly, it
celebrates a martial ethos

01:20:26.240 --> 01:20:31.240
and a particular class ethos

01:20:33.492 --> 01:20:35.900
but that already, from its very beginnings

01:20:35.900 --> 01:20:38.950
it is telling you that there
are other possibilities

01:20:38.950 --> 01:20:40.470
and it projects.

01:20:40.470 --> 01:20:41.830
My, what interests me

01:20:41.830 --> 01:20:44.060
is this projection to a better,

01:20:44.060 --> 01:20:49.060
a different future and maybe
a better future which I think,

01:20:49.740 --> 01:20:54.230
which has among other things.

01:20:54.230 --> 01:20:55.992
Women are in charge.

01:20:55.992 --> 01:21:00.189
I mean therefore it's totally utopian.

01:21:00.189 --> 01:21:04.727
That it is, that it's materially

01:21:06.856 --> 01:21:11.856
and technologically much advanced
and much more comfortable

01:21:13.688 --> 01:21:18.633
and much less given to war.

01:21:20.089 --> 01:21:21.548
And I think that,

01:21:21.548 --> 01:21:22.933
one of the things that interests me

01:21:22.933 --> 01:21:24.490
about the way that Milton
may close this tradition

01:21:24.490 --> 01:21:27.820
is that there is a certain
despair about this.

01:21:27.820 --> 01:21:30.060
He gives you two different versions

01:21:30.060 --> 01:21:33.800
but basically seem to be
living under the satanic regime

01:21:33.800 --> 01:21:35.323
and not under the idenic one.

01:21:37.580 --> 01:21:38.853
- [Man] Good, thanks David.

01:21:48.803 --> 01:21:49.928
- [Woman] Thanks so much David.

01:21:49.928 --> 01:21:51.627
- Yeah.
- This was just such a joy.

01:21:51.627 --> 01:21:53.430
And maybe I'm just bringing out

01:21:53.430 --> 01:21:55.190
what was implicit in the talk

01:21:55.190 --> 01:21:57.870
and what I've found so delightful

01:21:57.870 --> 01:22:01.890
but the idea that epic has encoded in it

01:22:01.890 --> 01:22:05.292
tropologically sort of
scenes of its own reception

01:22:05.292 --> 01:22:07.680
and I guess I'm wondering and again,

01:22:07.680 --> 01:22:09.530
gonna do the thing where I ask a question

01:22:09.530 --> 01:22:11.030
to which I don't know the answer.

01:22:11.030 --> 01:22:16.030
So much of this seems like
a literary history of epic

01:22:16.948 --> 01:22:17.781
in that you know,

01:22:17.781 --> 01:22:21.230
are these scenes of reading
in particular or you know,

01:22:21.230 --> 01:22:25.423
how do you account for this
originating in an oral form?

01:22:26.353 --> 01:22:27.364
What would?

01:22:27.364 --> 01:22:28.197
I mean perhaps that
actually makes more sense

01:22:28.197 --> 01:22:29.530
at the scene of hospitality

01:22:29.530 --> 01:22:32.287
being the kind of primary
scene of reception but.

01:22:33.573 --> 01:22:36.010
- Thank you Cathy.

01:22:36.010 --> 01:22:39.060
Yeah I mean, the.

01:22:39.060 --> 01:22:42.560
We assume that the Homeric poems

01:22:42.560 --> 01:22:46.040
were recited at events very much

01:22:46.040 --> 01:22:47.833
like the banquet in Scheria.

01:22:49.346 --> 01:22:52.703
You have a banquet and the
famously opening of book nine.

01:22:53.552 --> 01:22:56.570
This is what Plato hated.

01:22:56.570 --> 01:22:58.577
Odysseus says, "The best of things,

01:22:58.577 --> 01:23:01.747
"the very best of things
is when people all sit here

01:23:01.747 --> 01:23:04.967
"and are eating and feasting
and listening to poetry."

01:23:06.230 --> 01:23:09.670
And so it is giving us,

01:23:09.670 --> 01:23:11.960
it is clearly a scene of reception

01:23:11.960 --> 01:23:15.070
that's already there in the archaic world.

01:23:15.070 --> 01:23:18.360
By the time we get to Virgil,

01:23:18.360 --> 01:23:22.390
I mean we are probably
people are listening

01:23:22.390 --> 01:23:24.750
to epic in very much the same way.

01:23:24.750 --> 01:23:26.740
That they're probably, these are.

01:23:26.740 --> 01:23:31.600
It's being recited
orally probably to people

01:23:31.600 --> 01:23:33.943
and maybe after dinner.

01:23:35.270 --> 01:23:38.130
And, but it is this.

01:23:38.130 --> 01:23:40.222
We are sort of seeing,

01:23:40.222 --> 01:23:43.211
and I think this continues all the way.

01:23:43.211 --> 01:23:45.820
All the way through Paradise Lost,

01:23:45.820 --> 01:23:48.270
that these, that Raphael,

01:23:48.270 --> 01:23:51.200
Raphael's telling his narrations.

01:23:51.200 --> 01:23:55.130
That this is one of the
ways in which poetry,

01:23:55.130 --> 01:23:57.770
this long oral tradition is still there

01:23:57.770 --> 01:23:59.260
because when we talk about,

01:23:59.260 --> 01:24:02.503
people read things aloud in the,

01:24:04.090 --> 01:24:08.370
up until audiobooks.

01:24:08.370 --> 01:24:11.570
But the nights,

01:24:11.570 --> 01:24:14.260
one way to understand things like

01:24:14.260 --> 01:24:17.860
these interminable 17th century novels

01:24:18.705 --> 01:24:23.705
and very long 19th century
novels is that you know,

01:24:24.450 --> 01:24:26.380
every night after dinner,

01:24:26.380 --> 01:24:27.560
people are gathered together

01:24:27.560 --> 01:24:29.900
and somebody's sitting there reading.

01:24:29.900 --> 01:24:34.270
And that this is very much a
part of what sociability was.

01:24:34.270 --> 01:24:36.240
They didn't have television.

01:24:36.240 --> 01:24:40.150
So this is one of the
ways in which these novels

01:24:40.150 --> 01:24:41.903
are being produced.

01:24:43.306 --> 01:24:44.600
What interests me is the notion.

01:24:44.600 --> 01:24:47.400
I mean clearly this talk
has said that is that this,

01:24:47.400 --> 01:24:48.313
it's always.

01:24:49.160 --> 01:24:50.470
The audience is projected
as being a future.

01:24:50.470 --> 01:24:53.300
It's a posterity, it's a future audience

01:24:53.300 --> 01:24:55.163
that's being imagined.

01:24:56.322 --> 01:25:01.322
And also one that basically is
at considerable distance from

01:25:01.840 --> 01:25:06.840
this, from the world of the epic.

01:25:06.985 --> 01:25:10.067
The epic can imagine people
who don't really share

01:25:10.067 --> 01:25:15.053
some of its heroes values and right there.

01:25:18.730 --> 01:25:19.573
I, the.

01:25:22.106 --> 01:25:23.270
I guess the corresponding
moment in the Iliad

01:25:23.270 --> 01:25:26.510
is when we find Achilles singing

01:25:26.510 --> 01:25:31.366
the feats of heroes in his tent.

01:25:31.366 --> 01:25:32.250
That this is,

01:25:32.250 --> 01:25:35.423
that's how this stuff
is being transmitted.

01:25:36.560 --> 01:25:37.393
That's all.

01:25:41.750 --> 01:25:42.583
Yes.

01:25:47.440 --> 01:25:49.340
- Maybe I'll take us
to the final question.

01:25:49.340 --> 01:25:51.430
David, I want to ask you to maybe reflect

01:25:51.430 --> 01:25:53.500
about what is the consequence for

01:25:53.500 --> 01:25:57.024
a kind of longer literary
history of the talk you just gave

01:25:57.024 --> 01:25:58.290
because in part, what
you are talking about

01:25:58.290 --> 01:26:00.420
is a kind of dialectical relationship

01:26:00.420 --> 01:26:04.403
between a mercantile,
commercial world of the modern

01:26:04.403 --> 01:26:06.880
and thinking of Octavius

01:26:06.880 --> 01:26:08.420
and in Shakespeare's
"Antony and Cleopatra",

01:26:08.420 --> 01:26:11.740
the universal landlord
versus the epic boss

01:26:11.740 --> 01:26:15.223
to which is Antony who
will be defeated at Actium

01:26:15.223 --> 01:26:17.910
and I wonder if there's something about

01:26:17.910 --> 01:26:20.540
the dialectical nature of these in epic,

01:26:20.540 --> 01:26:23.070
the way in which epic
opens these wormholes

01:26:23.070 --> 01:26:25.240
that might make us think or rethink

01:26:25.240 --> 01:26:27.780
the kind of Bakhtinian
and Lukacsian difference

01:26:27.780 --> 01:26:28.950
between epic and novel.

01:26:28.950 --> 01:26:31.650
To think about at what
points does the novel,

01:26:31.650 --> 01:26:34.970
perhaps into the 19th and
20th century, even now.

01:26:34.970 --> 01:26:37.520
In its engagement with this dialectic,

01:26:37.520 --> 01:26:40.020
with this presentation of
the kind of future tense

01:26:40.020 --> 01:26:43.960
that is also retrospective is returning us

01:26:43.960 --> 01:26:47.200
to some of the kind of intellectual
work that epic is doing

01:26:47.200 --> 01:26:49.560
and the kind of cultural
place that epic has.

01:26:49.560 --> 01:26:54.050
Even if it's abandoning the
specific tropoligcal tracing

01:26:54.050 --> 01:26:55.573
that you did in this talk.

01:26:57.270 --> 01:26:59.300
- Well that's, I mean it's a huge question

01:26:59.300 --> 01:27:00.470
to which I'm not sure I can give

01:27:00.470 --> 01:27:04.160
a particularly satisfactory
answer but what I am trying.

01:27:04.160 --> 01:27:07.760
Yeah, I mean part of what
I'm clearly trying to say,

01:27:07.760 --> 01:27:10.543
that is, that.

01:27:12.721 --> 01:27:13.950
The Bakhtinian model

01:27:13.950 --> 01:27:17.233
is that the novel swallows up all genres.

01:27:18.630 --> 01:27:22.613
The Morettian model is that the
epic swallows up all genres.

01:27:23.580 --> 01:27:25.060
And that it's already,

01:27:25.060 --> 01:27:28.163
that and one way to see this is that the,

01:27:29.520 --> 01:27:34.520
that we already have
novellas in some ways,

01:27:34.570 --> 01:27:35.913
included within,

01:27:37.201 --> 01:27:40.727
preview with Renaissance epic but even,

01:27:40.727 --> 01:27:45.180
let's take the Ares and Aphrodite episode.

01:27:45.180 --> 01:27:47.553
Just that there are generic mixings

01:27:47.553 --> 01:27:49.593
that are already there in epic.

01:27:54.080 --> 01:27:55.870
Yes, I think that

01:27:58.262 --> 01:28:03.262
the, the question of where
does the mercantile world fit

01:28:10.000 --> 01:28:12.600
into, next.

01:28:12.600 --> 01:28:16.580
It is literally adjacent in the Odyssey

01:28:16.580 --> 01:28:20.396
to the world of the heroes.

01:28:20.396 --> 01:28:22.670
In the second part of the Odyssey,

01:28:22.670 --> 01:28:25.798
the stories of Phoenician traders are,

01:28:25.798 --> 01:28:30.510
and also of cretins who
are also seafaring people

01:28:31.480 --> 01:28:35.270
are all over the place and they you know,

01:28:35.270 --> 01:28:37.870
Odysseus makes up stories in which

01:28:37.870 --> 01:28:42.830
he is interacting with the
Phoenicians, with cretins.

01:28:42.830 --> 01:28:44.823
He is and,

01:28:47.139 --> 01:28:52.139
we recognize that there
is this whole other world

01:28:52.280 --> 01:28:56.210
of trade and commerce
that's, that's sitting there.

01:28:56.210 --> 01:28:57.293
But which is,

01:28:58.440 --> 01:29:03.440
which is not being enobled
through warfare and that is,

01:29:05.476 --> 01:29:07.073
that that seems to be what.

01:29:07.073 --> 01:29:09.250
I mean it is a dialectic
that's there from the beginning

01:29:09.250 --> 01:29:11.460
and if we want to say
that that is part of what,

01:29:11.460 --> 01:29:13.630
the work of the novel as a,

01:29:13.630 --> 01:29:15.590
particularly as a bourgeois form

01:29:15.590 --> 01:29:18.040
is that perhaps it does
reverse these things.

01:29:18.040 --> 01:29:20.000
Part of the problem also with Bakhtin

01:29:20.000 --> 01:29:23.558
just to spell it out is that when Bakhtin

01:29:23.558 --> 01:29:26.130
tries to talk about the novel,

01:29:26.130 --> 01:29:30.440
partly because of his own,

01:29:30.440 --> 01:29:32.680
his own political situation, whatever.

01:29:32.680 --> 01:29:34.000
He wants to talk about the novel

01:29:34.000 --> 01:29:35.980
as being something that
comes from the folk

01:29:35.980 --> 01:29:39.330
from down below and the
novel's a bourgeois form

01:29:39.330 --> 01:29:40.593
and that's,

01:29:40.593 --> 01:29:42.357
I mean Lukacs actually
has no problems with this.

01:29:42.357 --> 01:29:45.310
But it is, it's a bourgeois form

01:29:45.310 --> 01:29:48.700
and therefore it is trying to justify

01:29:48.700 --> 01:29:53.018
the existence of a class that
is not aristocratic and noble.

01:29:53.018 --> 01:29:55.640
Even though many of its protagonists

01:29:55.640 --> 01:29:57.703
will still be from the old aristocracy.

01:29:59.015 --> 01:30:03.360
So I'm not, I'm rambling
at this point but I am.

01:30:03.360 --> 01:30:06.151
But I think that one way to look at this

01:30:06.151 --> 01:30:07.560
is the same kind of reversal that epic

01:30:07.560 --> 01:30:09.660
is still looking at
things, to a certain extent

01:30:09.660 --> 01:30:14.113
from the side of the
dominant martial ethos.

01:30:15.456 --> 01:30:20.456
And that up to Milton, where there's this.

01:30:20.753 --> 01:30:22.966
Where the reversal really does take place

01:30:22.966 --> 01:30:25.640
and that the novel tends to,

01:30:25.640 --> 01:30:26.990
tends to follow Milton

01:30:26.990 --> 01:30:29.683
in wishing to find a virtuous bourgeoisie.

01:30:33.700 --> 01:30:34.533
Yeah.

01:30:44.151 --> 01:30:45.997
- Thank you so much David.

01:30:45.997 --> 01:30:48.810
I mean speaking of the dialectic,

01:30:48.810 --> 01:30:52.767
I was thinking that maybe Lukacs' point

01:30:55.020 --> 01:30:59.020
is not even so much a distinction between

01:30:59.020 --> 01:31:01.591
a roundedness of the epics

01:31:01.591 --> 01:31:04.993
and something else in the novel.

01:31:04.993 --> 01:31:06.070
That on the contrary,

01:31:06.070 --> 01:31:09.050
one could say nothing is more obsessed

01:31:09.050 --> 01:31:11.570
with another version of roundedness

01:31:11.570 --> 01:31:15.053
than the totality than
the novel for Lukacs.

01:31:16.132 --> 01:31:21.132
It seems rather than whatever
we really had to sing about,

01:31:22.060 --> 01:31:23.200
the epics.

01:31:23.200 --> 01:31:28.200
His idea was that the
epic had a ground beyond

01:31:28.840 --> 01:31:30.690
or before itself.

01:31:30.690 --> 01:31:33.680
What is really epic for him
is not so much the epics

01:31:33.680 --> 01:31:35.170
but the epic world.

01:31:35.170 --> 01:31:36.130
The world in which

01:31:36.130 --> 01:31:38.010
the epic originates.
- Yep.

01:31:38.010 --> 01:31:42.550
- As the epic and the
novel as that totality

01:31:42.550 --> 01:31:44.600
which doesn't have an epic world.

01:31:44.600 --> 01:31:47.820
- Yeah though I.
- And just one last point

01:31:47.820 --> 01:31:49.260
of those.

01:31:49.260 --> 01:31:52.730
I mean Lukacs wrote his piece as a preface

01:31:52.730 --> 01:31:57.530
to a book he has never written
on Dostoevsky and he was,

01:31:57.530 --> 01:32:00.720
his idea was a return of the,

01:32:00.720 --> 01:32:04.830
of kind of an epic
constellation in modernity.

01:32:04.830 --> 01:32:06.900
So my question would be,

01:32:06.900 --> 01:32:10.320
do we just think that
this brings something

01:32:10.320 --> 01:32:15.320
to the possibility of a
literary history of the epics?

01:32:16.262 --> 01:32:18.410
- Well okay.

01:32:18.410 --> 01:32:19.590
I mean this, the.

01:32:25.525 --> 01:32:30.442
I mean Lukacs is writing in a
Hegelian tradition and Hegel,

01:32:34.015 --> 01:32:37.030
in the Aesthetics says that really,

01:32:37.030 --> 01:32:41.000
there are like four epics
that actually are epics.

01:32:41.000 --> 01:32:42.914
They're the Homeric poems,

01:32:42.914 --> 01:32:44.640
he's not quite sure about the Odyssey.

01:32:44.640 --> 01:32:46.275
The Homeric poems.

01:32:46.275 --> 01:32:50.990
The Mahabharata, the
Ramayana, I'm sorry five.

01:32:50.990 --> 01:32:52.400
And the Sit.

01:32:52.400 --> 01:32:55.060
Those are the four.

01:32:55.060 --> 01:32:57.733
By which he, and excuse me for a second.

01:33:03.640 --> 01:33:08.153
And because Germany needs an
epic, maybe the Nibelungenlied.

01:33:09.900 --> 01:33:11.260
But that's it.

01:33:11.260 --> 01:33:15.950
So epic is very narrowly
defined as being the,

01:33:15.950 --> 01:33:20.200
and also for Hegel, it's
the primitiveness of Homer

01:33:20.200 --> 01:33:23.040
which is all important to the world

01:33:23.040 --> 01:33:26.300
and also the idea is Hegel knows

01:33:26.300 --> 01:33:29.940
that the person who wrote, composed.

01:33:29.940 --> 01:33:32.300
Excuse me, composed the Homeric poems

01:33:32.300 --> 01:33:34.270
is from a later period.

01:33:34.270 --> 01:33:36.590
But this person seems to have managed

01:33:36.590 --> 01:33:39.200
to completely absorb himself back into

01:33:39.200 --> 01:33:42.860
this earlier heroic period
and therefore he is,

01:33:42.860 --> 01:33:45.510
and therefore he and his characters

01:33:45.510 --> 01:33:50.420
feel absolutely no kinds of
questioning or disconnection

01:33:50.420 --> 01:33:52.882
with the world in which they live.

01:33:52.882 --> 01:33:55.230
Well, it becomes a problem

01:33:55.230 --> 01:33:56.077
when you start reading the Iliad

01:33:56.077 --> 01:33:58.040
and the first person you meet is Achilles

01:33:58.040 --> 01:34:02.210
who's profoundly you know, disconnected

01:34:02.210 --> 01:34:06.623
with his community and
with the world around him.

01:34:07.610 --> 01:34:11.610
But this is a kind of romantic world

01:34:11.610 --> 01:34:14.570
that is imagined by Hegel
and the very largest

01:34:14.570 --> 01:34:17.210
that Lukacs repeats in the opening

01:34:17.210 --> 01:34:20.300
of The Theory of the Novel
and that the hero is.

01:34:20.300 --> 01:34:23.093
It's a world where everyone
is at home in the world.

01:34:24.160 --> 01:34:27.976
Where there's no
dissociation of sensibility,

01:34:27.976 --> 01:34:30.750
to use Elliot's term.

01:34:30.750 --> 01:34:33.053
But I mean, that's just.

01:34:33.053 --> 01:34:36.730
It's clearly not right about these poems

01:34:36.730 --> 01:34:38.310
and it is part of a,

01:34:38.310 --> 01:34:40.820
it's part of a certain
kind of romantic nostalgia

01:34:40.820 --> 01:34:41.920
which is not un,

01:34:41.920 --> 01:34:45.803
which is partly a nostalgia
for the Ancien Regime

01:34:45.803 --> 01:34:49.590
even by people who were
probably supporters

01:34:49.590 --> 01:34:51.950
of the French Revolution but nonetheless

01:34:51.950 --> 01:34:54.610
have a kind of deep nostalgia
for this and that's where

01:34:54.610 --> 01:34:57.963
a certain kind of romantic
primitivism comes in.

01:34:59.220 --> 01:35:02.390
But it seems to be, this is just not.

01:35:02.390 --> 01:35:05.610
When one reads the poems,
this is simply not the case.

01:35:05.610 --> 01:35:07.760
It's nice for theory
but theory is you know,

01:35:07.760 --> 01:35:10.360
when you then actually look at the poems,

01:35:10.360 --> 01:35:11.693
they don't work that way.

01:35:12.680 --> 01:35:17.140
So that I think, one way is
that the notion of roundedness

01:35:17.140 --> 01:35:18.470
and of,

01:35:18.470 --> 01:35:22.740
well more of being at
home in the heroic world

01:35:22.740 --> 01:35:27.740
is what Hegel wishes and again,

01:35:27.983 --> 01:35:30.930
it's partly also nostalgia for faith,

01:35:30.930 --> 01:35:35.930
nostalgia for the imminence
of the divine in the world.

01:35:39.670 --> 01:35:42.690
It's already called into question by epic

01:35:42.690 --> 01:35:44.330
from its beginning.

01:35:44.330 --> 01:35:46.500
And that, and I just
think that that's what one

01:35:46.500 --> 01:35:50.200
has to just sort of see that in that way,

01:35:50.200 --> 01:35:52.880
human nature did not change

01:35:52.880 --> 01:35:57.230
in the way that literary,

01:35:57.230 --> 01:35:58.770
that this version of literary history

01:35:58.770 --> 01:36:00.170
would want to make us think.

01:36:02.914 --> 01:36:04.477
Thank you.
- All this talk of banquets

01:36:04.477 --> 01:36:07.420
makes me think we should
move on to our reception

01:36:07.420 --> 01:36:10.220
in Phelps Hall which
is on the fourth floor

01:36:10.220 --> 01:36:12.350
but please join me in thanking David

01:36:12.350 --> 01:36:16.930
for a fantastic lecture.
